3. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-27082-112
T1  - The exploration of the lived experience of Black women directors in student affairs leading others during the COVID-19 pandemic.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - King, Coretta L
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - This study explored the perceived organizational attitudes, behaviors, and practices that undermined the health and productivity of Black women leaders in student affairs during the COVID-19 pandemic. This study used a qualitative research approach that enabled respondents to share their perceptions on organizational support for the purpose of enhancing the personnel experience for Black women leaders within student affairs and relatable professions. The methods of research asked the following questions of Black women directors currently or formerly employed in student affairs during the COVID-19 pandemic: How do Black women leaders in student affairs experience organizational support within their daily operations during the COVID-19 global pandemic? How do Black women leading in student affairs perceive differences in their lived experiences compared to their peers during the COVID-19 global pandemic? How do Black women leaders in student affairs perceive the organization's support during the COVID-19 global pandemic? What organizational behaviors within student affairs do they believe helped reinforce organizational wellness for Black women leaders during the COVID-19 global pandemic? The findings of the data shared from the interviews can be summed into three issues: (a) lack of perceived empathy and compassion in actionable leadership with an over-emphasis on work as usual, (b) perceived insufficient checks and balances for performance management for director leadership and above, and (c) the need for re-imagining the distribution of work in student affairs going forward. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
