34. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-12027-143
T1  - Feasibility of implementing C-SSRS in primary care.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Roth, Danielle Marie
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Purpose: The purpose of this quality improvement project was to educate Mayo Clinic staff on the barriers and benefits of C-SSRS screening tool in an attempt to gain their support and intent to adopt the C-SSRS into practice in primary care for screening for all patients with a behavioral health diagnosis. Background: In the United States, suicide is one of the top reasons for death, with close to 50,000 people dying from suicide in 2020. The number of adults who acknowledge they have experienced suicidal thoughts is a little over 12 million Americans. Most patients who attempt suicide have seen their primary care provider within the last year, with close to half in the last month. The identification of patients who are experiencing social ideations is critical to patient safety and the C-SRRS tool is a validated and reliable screening tool for identification of suicidal thoughts. The author presented benefits, barriers, and feasibility strategies to a team of content experts with the goal of gaining their support and intent to adopt presentation recommendations into practice. Methods: The quality improvement project was designed using the Model for Improvement and PDSA cycle. Participants were recruited via email to view a live, web-based presentation sharing best practices from the literature when implementing C-SSRS tool in primary care. Participants completed a pretest and post intervention survey to measure any change in perceived feasibility, barriers, benefits, and intention to adopt recommendations into practice. Results: Nine staff members, from various disciplines including psychiatry and primary care participated in the project. All nine participants completed the pretest and post intervention survey. The results showed an increase in perceived feasibility, benefits, support, and intention to adopt. Conclusion: The results of the project show the presentation was successful. The project shows that perceived feasibility, benefits, support, and intention to adopt the C-SSRS can be improved by an evidence-based presentation. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
