42. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-12026-292
T1  - Computer anxiety and work-related computer-based test performance: Employee perceptions.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Sizer, Larry Darnell
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - This qualitative descriptive study was conducted to better understand how older and younger experienced contracting professionals described work-related computer anxiety and its influence on work-related computer-based test performance. The theoretical foundations supporting the study were Bandura's social cognitive theory and Eysenck and Calvo's processing efficiency theory. The study was guided by two research questions: How do younger and older contracting professionals describe work-related computer anxiety? How do older and younger contracting professionals describe the influence of work-related computer anxiety on work-related computer-based test performance? Sources of data were the Computer Anxiety Rating Scale survey, Contracting Officer Demo Test results, and open-ended interview questions from 21 contracting professionals. Descriptive statistics were generated for both the CODT results and CARS; thematic analysis of narrative data resulted in six themes. 1) Regardless of age, experience within the field and exposure to technology increases comfort with the computer and lessens anxiety. 2) Computer anxiety was experienced by older and younger participants. 3) More participants in both age groups preferred testing via computer. 4) Computer anxiety influenced computer-based test performance by impacting ability to concentrate or focus. 5) Preparation for work-related computer-based tests can increase pass rates and decrease computer anxiety. 6) Computer anxiety was exacerbated by the timed format of the test. process was used to analyze open-ended data. Allowing more time to complete the computer-based test and familiarizing test takers with the computer-based test format are recommended. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
