N2  - Culture is ubiquitous in the therapeutic encounter, impacting the formation of the alliance, the strength of client-therapist match, and the efficacy of treatment. The role that the culture of the therapist plays in the therapeutic encounter is important. The construct of culture encompasses a broad array of client and therapist factors that are individual, collectivistic, and shared at a broader communal level. This chapter discusses the construct of culture and summarizes research on the impact that the culture of the client and of the therapist can have on therapy process and outcome. It highlights the importance of multicultural competencies as fundamental therapist skills. The chapter reviews research on efforts to adapt psychotherapy to specific cultural identities, with a focus on adaptations related to race and ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, and religion and spirituality. It illustrates the clinical process and implications of adapting psychotherapy to cultural identity with a clinical example. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
