85. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-12209-001
T1  - Retracted: Sustained auditory attentional load decreases audiovisual integration in older and younger adults.
T3  - Journal of Neural Transplantation, Journal of Neural Transplantation & Plasticity
A2  - Neural Plasticity
A2  - Y.Ren, Y.Hou, J.Huang, et al.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Reports the retraction of "Sustained auditory attentional load decreases audiovisual integration in older and younger adults" by Yanna Ren, Yawei Hou, Jiayu Huang, Fanghong Li, Tao Wang, Yanling Ren and Weiping Yang (Neural Plasticity, 2021[Jun][17], Vol 2021[4516133]). This article has been retracted by Hindawi following an investigation undertaken by the publisher. This investigation has uncovered evidence of one or more indicators of systematic manipulation of the publication process, as listed in the retraction. The presence of these indicators undermines their confidence in the integrity of the article's content and they cannot, therefore, vouch for its reliability. Wiley and Hindawi regrets that the usual quality checks did not identify several issues before publication and have since put additional measures in place to safeguard research integrity. They wish to credit their own Research Integrity and Research Publishing teams and anonymous and named external researchers and research integrity experts for contributing to this investigation. The corresponding author, as the representative of all authors, has been given the opportunity to register their agreement or disagreement to this retraction. Wiley and Hindawi have kept a record of any response received. (The following abstract of the original article appeared in record 2021-65644-001). The modulation of attentional load on the perception of auditory and visual information has been widely reported; however, whether attentional load alters audiovisual integration (AVI) has seldom been investigated. Here, to explore the effect of sustained auditory attentional load on AVI and the effects of aging, nineteen older and 20 younger adults performed an AV discrimination task with a rapid serial auditory presentation task competing for attentional resources. The results showed that responses to audiovisual stimuli were significantly faster than those to auditory and visual stimuli (AV > V >= A, all p < 0:001), and the younger adults were significantly faster than the older adults under all attentional load conditions (all p < 0:001). The analysis of the race model showed that AVI was decreased and delayed with the addition of auditory sustained attention (no load > load 1 > load 2 > load 3 > load 4) for both older and younger adults. In addition, AVI was lower and more delayed in older adults than in younger adults in all attentional load conditions. These results suggested that auditory sustained attentional load decreased AVI and that AVI was reduced in older adults. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
