86. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-12139-001
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.childyouth.2023.107142
T1  - Corrigendum to "Empirical research on parental alienation: A descriptive literature review" [Child. Youth Serv. Rev. 119 (2020) 105572].
A1  - Marques, T. M
A1  - Narciso, I
A1  - Ferreira, L. C
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Reports an error in "Empirical research on parental alienation: A descriptive literature review" by T. M. Marques, I. Narciso and L. C. Ferreira (Children and Youth Services Review, 2020[Dec], Vol 119[105572]). In the original article, statements presented on p. 8 constitute incorrect information that may lead to misunderstandings regarding the parental alienation theory. These two sentences (provided in this corrigendum) may convey an incorrect idea and the authors want to correct this misinformation by deleting the sentences. (The following abstract of the original article appeared in record 2020-97615-001). Scientific literature has pointed to a growing body of empirical studies that contribute to an accurate mapping of parental alienation. This descriptive literature review of empirical research on parental alienation - in peer-reviewed scientific journals between January 2000 and December 2018 - seeks to characterize the research methodologies and to provide a summary of the main research themes. Several academic databases (B-On: Online Knowledge Library-Search, Ovid, ProQuest, Web of Knowledge, PsycINFO and Google Scholar) were systematically searched and the review followed the PRISMA guidelines for reporting systematic reviews and meta-analyses. Forty-three studies were included, the majority of them following a quantitative, retrospective and cross-sectional design. Parental alienation was mainly associated with divorce and child custody dispute contexts. Results also revealed the main themes targeted by empirical research: Development and use of assessment measures for parental alienation; Parental alienation patterns; Validation of the parental alienation syndrome construct; Parental alienation impact; Parental alienation viewed as child abuse; Professionals' voices and maps on parental alienation. Limitations and recommendations for future research on parental alienation are discussed, highlighting common themes and research gaps. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
