T1  - Retracted: Effects of acupuncture and rehabilitation training on limb movement and living ability of patients with hemiplegia after stroke.
A2  - Behavioural Neurology
A2  - J.Zhang, Y.Mu, Y.Zhang
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Reports the retraction of "Effects of acupuncture and rehabilitation training on limb movement and living ability of patients with hemiplegia after stroke" by Juhua Zhang, Yingmei Mu and Yunxia Zhang (Behavioural Neurology, 2022[Apr][26], Vol 2022[2032093]). This article has been retracted by Hindawi following an investigation undertaken by the publisher. This investigation has uncovered evidence of one or more of the several indicators of systematic manipulation of the publication process presented in retraction. Wiley and Hindawi regrets that the usual quality checks did not identify the several issues before publication and have since put additional measures in place to safeguard research integrity. The corresponding author, as the representative of all authors, has been given the opportunity to register their agreement or disagreement to this retraction. We have kept a record of any response received. (The following abstract of the original article appeared in record 2022-76997-001). Stroke is a disease with a high disability rate, having a serious impact on that patient's working and daily survival quality and bringing economic burden to the family and society. Patients with stroke hemiplegia are mostly tetraplegic and have difficulty regulating their balance, and their long-term symmetry has been destroyed. The application in the rehabilitation process of acupuncture in patients with hemorrhagic stroke may produce unexpected effects. It is very effective to study the effect of acupuncture combined with rehabilitation training on limb movement and patient survival. It is very helpful in improving normal motor function and normal life, increasing joint mobility and muscle strength, and reducing muscle tension. In this paper, it is found that the observational group has a complication rate of 2.13%, in contrast to 17.02% as in the group of control, and the pin-prick combined with a rehabilitative training makes a significant improvement to the patients. This study provides suggestions for the study to investigate acupuncture combined with recovery exercise on limb movement and living capacities of people with stroke paraparesis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
