141. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-12020-082
T1  - The influence of generational conflict on person-supervisor fit.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Epps, William A Jr.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Today's workforce consists of multiple generations. Managers face managing individuals with different backgrounds and stories impacted by their social and historical events, impacting the development of their attitudes, motivations, and views of the world around them. These differences can create conflicts. Although the research conducted on generational conflict, there is a gap in research on the American corporate culture, specifically the banking and financial industry, which has its own unique culture. This qualitative, descriptive study aimed to understand how generational conflict influences the effectiveness of person-supervisor relationships and how this impacts financial sector employees' perceptions. With data collected from two qualitative questionnaires and a focus group discussion, the researcher analyzed 1160 instances of the code amongst 204 excerpts and identified six themes aligned with the two research questions. The researcher intended to develop strategies that can be implemented by managers/supervisors to prevent and mitigate generational conflicts with employees of a different generational cohort. The participants comprised twenty employees and managers/supervisors in the United States specializing in operations and project/program management within a financial sector corporation. Managers/Supervisors had at least two direct reports. Using triangulation, the researcher identified and provided three recommendations to decrease generational conflicts impacting person-supervisor relationships and strategies to create efficient person-supervisor relationships when there are differing generations. These recommendations consisted of developing and executing a plan for transforming senior management to be inclusive of different generations, utilizing a servant leadership style, and promoting direct and effective communication with various specific training. In the end, the researcher provided five areas of future research related to person-supervisor fit and the following areas: racial identity, impression management, a focus on Generation Z, organizational justice, and leader-member exchange. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
