142. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-12020-054
T1  - Relationship between parenting and parental distress: parental anxiety and quality of life in parents of children with autism and neurotypical children.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Mongold, Samantha
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - There has been a lack of research regarding the effects of parenting a child with autism on parental anxiety and quality of life (QOL), while looking at the impact of the parent-child relationship. This research studied these effects as they relate between parenting group: parents of children with autism, and parents of neurotypical children. The design of the research was nonexperimental, cross-sectional, and quantitative. The analyses included multivariate analysis of variances (MANOVAs), simple linear regressions, an independent samples t-test, and a multivariate analysis of covariance (MANCOVA). The findings indicate an effect of parenting children with autism on only general QOL, and when looking within that group, those with higher state and trait anxiety were reporting lower general quality of life. Also, the reports on the parent-child relationship were equal amongst the parenting groups, though when parents observed more support, more satisfaction, and they reported they communicated with the child more, they tended to report higher trait anxiety. Regarding general QOL, parents of children with ASD typically reported lower general QOL when reporting the more support they receive, communication they have, and involvement they take part in with the child; conversely, these parents report higher general QOL when they feel more satisfied in parenting. Post hoc analyses reflected parents of children with ASD report higher levels of trait anxiety, which affects all domains of QOL. The research implies that parents of children with autism experience more distress than parents of neurotypical children, which may impact the symptoms of the child, therefore there is an importance on including the parents in interventions with the child. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
