166. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-00967-130
T1  - Counselors in a foreign land: The impact of acculturation on counselor self-efficacy and job satisfaction.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Solomon, Susana
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Cross-cultural psychology shows a considerable connection between a person's behavioral development and cultural context. This relationship may be due to acculturation, which forces individuals to adapt to new cultural provisions despite growing up in their heritage culture. This results in acculturative stress, which lowers self-efficacy, and, in turn, job satisfaction. This extends to counselors, specifically, foreign-born counselors (FBCs) and U.S. counselors (USCs). This study investigated acculturation and acculturative stress's effects on counselor self-efficacy and job satisfaction. The work focused on whether counselors' birthplaces predicted overall job satisfaction after considering acculturative stress, self-efficacy, and acculturation. The methodology incorporated a nonexperimental correlational design, relying on four instruments: COSE-TR for self-efficacy, VIA for acculturation, RASI for acculturative stress, and MSQ for job satisfaction. The study found that counselors' linkage with their own/heritage cultures boosts self-efficacy and heightens work satisfaction. There was also a more significant relationship between FBCs and acculturative stress than USCs. Despite showing nonsignificant effects of acculturative stress and mainstream culture on counselors, this research identified missing links in current studies. Future studies should investigate the impact of a counselor's work experience on their acculturation and acculturative stress and how it influences self-efficacy and job satisfaction. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
