Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Comments on an article, Data reproducibility issues and their potential impact on conclusions from evidence syntheses of randomized controlled trials in sleep medicine by C. Xu et al. (2022). Systematic reviews and meta-analyses represent two critical pillars of scientific evidence accumulation and synthesis. They improve the precision of quantitative estimates and answer questions at a scale that individual studies cannot answer. Importantly, they are instrumental in arbitrating between studies that seemingly provide conflicting results. In many ways, systematic reviews and meta-analyses represent the gold standard for how knowledge should be aggregated. However, quite far from this ideal is the procedural reality of evidence synthesis: The process of data extraction for systematic reviews and meta-analysis is performed by hand, thereby introducing possible sources of error. Indeed, in systematic reviews of therapeutical interventions, reproducible research practices are rarely used . (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
