195. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-00966-155
T1  - An exploration of the sense of belonging among medical students at one academic medical center in the southeastern united states.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Scott, Amanda Lucas
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Introduction: Attending medical school is regarded to be a highly stressful and socially isolating experience. The effects of high stress levels include depression, burnout, substance abuse, thoughts of dropping out of school, suicidal ideation, diminished academic performance, increased risk of making medical errors, and decreased patient empathy. Medical schools have implemented strategies and resources to improve the well-being of medical students such as counseling and self-care courses. A sense of belonging is associated with improved resilience to stress and anxiety and academic engagement. By understanding student perceptions of their sense of belonging, findings from this study will provide the University of Mississippi Medical Center (UMMC) and other academic medical centers insight on how to foster a sense of belonging for its medical students. Methods: This sequential explanatory mixed methods study aimed to observe student interpersonal interactions and explore student and faculty perceptions of UMMC medical students' sense of belonging to gain a better understanding of responses on biennial student satisfaction surveys. The study consisted of three distinct phases. Phase one quantitative data were comprised of responses to seven items related to belonging on four years of student satisfaction survey data collected in 2016, 2018, 2020, and 2022. Chi-square tests were conducted to determine a relationship between medical students' sense of belonging and specific demographic factors. Trend analysis was conducted to identify trends in medical students' sense of belonging from four years of student satisfaction surveys. Phase two qualitative data were comprised of journal observations of medical student interactive phenomena within the UMMC School of Medicine (SOM). Open, axial, and selective coding was performed on the data to identify emerging themes. Phase three qualitative data were comprised of student and faculty study participants' responses to questions asked in semi-structured interviews. Findings from phase one and phase two were included in the interview guides, allowing participants to explain and inform the findings from phase one and phase two. Results: Quantitative findings revealed that, of the population of medical students who responded to student satisfaction surveys, an average of 80% of UMMC reported high levels of belonging to the SOM, and 79% of medical student respondents report high levels to the UMMC community. Findings also revealed that female medical students reported lower levels of belonging than males in 2018 and 2022 and Black students reported lower levels of belonging than other race/ethnicities in 2018, 2020, and 2022. Qualitative data from phase two revealed that students were comfortable interacting with each other and with the faculty and administration within the SOM. Findings from phase three revealed students who perceive having a sense of belonging to the SOM describe belongingness in three key themes: community, cooperation, and connection. Themes emerging from qualitative findings from phase three were the same themes identified in phase two, along with a fourth labeled: challenges. Conclusion: Findings of this study suggest two areas of opportunity which, if addressed, could foster a sense of belonging for medical students, possibly improving their overall well-being and academic motivation. These specific areas were: feeling excluded from certain groups and an unwillingness or inability to access support. To address these identified areas, the U-Belong Here outreach initiative was developed. This initiative includes a presentation, U-Belong Here: Medical Students and the Sense of Belonging, to share best practices for fostering a sense of belonging for medical students with other medical school faculty and administrators during an annual conference. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
