210. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2024-01907-002
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S2215-0366%2823%2900253-5
T1  - Time to rethink behaviours that challenge in people with intellectual disabilities.
A1  - Hassiotis, Angela
A2  - Branford, D, Shankar, R, Groves, L, Jones, C, Welham, A, Hamilton, A, Liew, A, Richards, C, Lunsky, Y, Lin, E, Balogh, R, Klein-Geltink, J, Wilton, AS, Kurdyak, P, McCarthy, J, Hemmings, C, Kravariti, E, et al., NICE, Reid, AH, Royal College of Psychiatrists, Smith, J, Baksh, RA, Hassiotis, A, et al., Stringaris, A, Vidal-Ribas, P, Brotman, MA, Leibenluft, E
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Comments on an article by Laura Groves et al. (see record 2024-01907-009). The article by Laura Groves and colleagues is a very welcome contribution to the literature, as it reaches some important conclusions about the relative effectiveness of pharmacological and psychosocial interventions for behaviours that challenge. Often, behaviours that challenge can be comorbid with mental disorders, which might be unrecognised given the often atypical presentation of symptoms of mental illness in this population. Using these findings, clinicians can better justify their reasoning and actions in their discussions with family members, paid carers, and people with intellectual disabilities or autism when establishing a treatment plan. Groves and colleagues also provide some positive news about the effectiveness of both types of interventions for all topographies of behaviours that challenge, for instance, suggesting that antipsychotics could be of benefit in the management of aggression based on the evidence available to date. The paper raises many concerns, such as the quality of reporting of the included clinical trials, the lack of clear descriptions of the psychosocial interventions or content of treatment as usual, and the overall small number of available randomised controlled trials. Groves and colleagues provide a well argued case for a renewed focus on examining our stance on behaviours that challenge, which can ultimately lead to skilled therapeutic input and more effective resource use. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
