T1  - Association between olfactory dysfunction and motor subtypes in Parkinson's disease: Are non-tremor-dominant subtypes really uncorrelated to olfactory impairment?
T3  - Italian Journal of Neurological Sciences
A1  - Frau, Claudia
A1  - Masala, Carla
A1  - Solla, Paolo
A1  - Ercoli, Tommaso
A1  - Defazio, Giovanni
AI  - Masala, Carla; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5272-8421
AI  - Solla, Paolo; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2982-0665
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Comments on the articles by F. Nabizadeh et al. (see record 2022-75908-017) and Olfactory dysfunction and striatal dopamine transporter binding in motor subtypes of Parkinson's disease (2022). The two articles by Nabizadeh and colleagues investigates respectively the association between olfactory impairment and three motor subtypes of Parkinson's disease (PD) such as tremor-dominant (TD), postural instability with gait difficulty (PIGD), and indeterminate subtypes, nd possible different associations between olfactory dysfunction and striatal dopaminergic neuronal loss among the same motor PD subtypes. Although the working hypotheses appear convincing, their conclusions raise several issues from a methodological point of view. In these two papers, authors analyzed data obtained from the Parkinson's Progression Markers Initiative (PPMI), which is an open-access data set projected with the aim to study biomarkers, improve knowledge about PD, and encourage disease-modifying therapies trials. According to their results, in the frst paper, they concluded that olfactory dysfunction was significantly correlated with declined motor functions only in TD patients, while no significant correlations were observed in patients with non-tremor-dominant forms (PIGD and indeterminate subtypes) and healthy subjects. Therefore, considering the low sample size of these last two groups, it appears clear that the statistical inferences are necessarily limited, undermining the conclusions on these two subtypes. In the latter paper, the authors concluded that the olfactory dysfunction was correlated with dopamine transporter activity in striatal nuclei only in the TD group. The problem is that among the recruited 464 drug-naive PD patients, 387 were TD patients but only 41 patients were PIGD, and 36 patients were indeterminate subtypes. The same authors recognized that the number of patients with the non-tremor-dominant (PIGD and intermediate) subtypes was less than TD, probably unbalancing the results, because of the highly significant between-group difference which may explain the absence of correlation in PIGD and indeterminate group. This finding plays a crucial role particularly in drug-naive PD patients where future treatments might be able to modify the course of the disease. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
