T1  - Complexity is the simple truth about Alzheimer's disease.
A1  - Frisoni, Giovanni B
A2  - Frisoni, GB, Altomare, D, Thal, DR, et al., Hardy, JA, Higgins, GA, Jagust, WJ, Teunissen, CE, DeCarli, C, Kraler, S, Libby, P, Evans, PC, et al., Livingston, G, Huntley, J, Sommerlad, A, et al., Trojanowski, JQ, van Dyck, CH, Swanson, CJ, Aisen, P, et al., Zheng, X, Tang, Y, Yang, Q, et al.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Comments on an article by W. J. Jagust et al. (see record 2024-01908-020). agust and colleagues propose a more complex pathophysiological model than the canonical amyloid cascade hypothesis for the development and progression of cognitive impairment and dementia, which comprises factors such as inflammation, vascular changes, proteinopathies other than amyloid beta and tau, and genetic and acquired resistance and resilience to molecular pathology. They further suggest that the relative contribution of the amyloid pathway to cognitive impairment and dementia might vary in different patients, in relation to coexisting pathophysiological processes. Jagust and colleagues outline Jagust and colleagues outline six strategies for future work, which can be summarised as the recruitment of more diverse clinical trial cohorts, the use of neurodegeneration markers (MRI and [18F]fluorodeoxyglucose-PET) as proxies of non-Alzheimer's disease pathological findings in research studies, the use of quantitative rather than dichotomic biomarkers to stratify patients in trials, the study of the dynamics of clinical outcomes over the disease course with specific attention to the earliest phases, the implementation of clinical trials combining drugs and lifestyle interventions, and the promotion of data sharing between academia and industry Although Jagust and colleagues do not explicitly say so, the pathophysiological scenario that they envisage for sporadic Alzheimer's disease is reminiscent of the scenario for vascular diseases. he clinical implications and predictions of the complex or probabilistic version of the amyloid cascade are substantially different from the implications and predictions of the canonical version (table) and will not appeal to either radical baptists or tauists. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
