T1  - Performance of a prediction model of suicide attempts across race and ethnicity innovative prevention strategies are need.
T3  - A.M.A. Archives of General Psychiatry, Archives of General Psychiatry
A1  - Papini, Santiago
A1  - Hsin, Honor
A1  - Kipnis, Patricia
A1  - Liu, Vincent X
A1  - Lu, Yun
A1  - Sterling, Stacy A
A1  - Iturralde, Esti
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This study examines whether disparities exist in the prediction of suicide attempts, which are far more common and have detrimental effects on individuals and health care systems, even when nonfatal. This diagnostic/prognostic study followed Transparent Reporting of a Multivariable Prediction Model for Individual Prognosis or Diagnosis (TRIPOD) reporting guidelines for prediction model validation. A previously developed logistic regression prediction model of fatal and nonfatal suicide attempts was applied to an independent cohort of outpatient mental health encounters at Kaiser Permanente Northern California between October 1, 2016, and September 30, 2017, for patients older than 12 years. There were 1 408 682 encounters among 254 777 unique patients with mean (SD) age of 40.7 (18.7) years, including 164 920 women (64.7%), 89 857 men (35.3%), and 35 267 individuals with Medicare coverage (13.8%). The suicide-attempt model achieved excellent overall classification across all racial and ethnic groups. This contrasts with prior findings with a suicide-death model, where authors posited that poorer performance for some racial and ethnic groups may have resulted from a number of factors, including systemic barriers to utilization,practitioner bias, institutional discrimination,outcome misclassification, and low statistical power to detect race-specific risk factors given the rarity of the outcome. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
