288. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-93852-125
T1  - Dynamic, directional, and heterogeneous functional connectivity of the human brain: A neuroimaging study.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Zhang, Gemeng
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Functional network connectivity (FNC) within the human brain as measured by functional neuroimaging data has attracted increasing attention and has been widely used to study brain development and mental disorders. A major focus of the research is analyzing the properties of functional connectivity (FC), including its temporal dynamic property, spatial directionality, and individual variation. The projects contain three sub-projects: assessing the individual variability of the whole brain FC in the group (age, gender, experiment paradigms), estimating dynamic FNC patterns from resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rs-fMRI) time series, and detecting abnormal directed FC in schizophrenia (SZ) patients.To address challenges in the current problem setting, this thesis proposes novel analytical models/pipelines for each of the projects. In the first project, we investigate the individual variance in the group analysis of FC in a unified linear mixed effects modeling(LMM) framework. In the second project, we focus on the windowless dynamic functional connectivity estimation using rs-fMRI data in a high-dimensional setting, in which the number of brain regions is less than the number of observations. In the third project, we focus on the directed acyclic graph(DAG) estimation using task fMRI data when the sample size is limited but the potential group structure is favorable.Through simulation studies and large neuroimaging data sets, the proposed analysis schemes are validated with identical findings from previous studies. By highlighting the prominent brain regions and connections, it can lead to a new understanding of brain function. In addition, we follow a reproducible and open-source strategy and all of our proposed methods and tools are widely available to the scientific community via the release of data and codes. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
