351. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-85470-116
T1  - Attending to soul wounds at the end of life: Neurodecolonizing practices and perspectives for palliative and end of life care.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Snyder, Bradley Elias
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This dissertation explores ways to ease existential suffering for people nearing the end-of-life by supporting them to reconnect to the healing practices of their ancestors. The concepts of existential suffering, epigenetics, soul wounds, ancestral healing, and neurodecolonization are brought together to introduce a new theory-attending to soul wounds at the end of life, which identifies both a problem and a solution that have not been sufficiently discussed in hospice and palliative care literature. This theory posits that a cause of existential suffering at the end of life is unresolved intergenerational trauma, or Soul Wounds, passed on from one's ancestors through epigenetics. A solution to healing these soul wounds is neurodecolonization, which invites one to reclaim and practice healing traditions and rituals of one's ancestors. Due to breaks, dilutions, and erasures from traditional practices, exemplified here by the impacts of colonization and the violent spread of monotheistic religions, 11 elders, healers, and wisdom keepers from different traditions around the world were invited to share their practices and perspectives about how to support people nearing the end of life. The hope is that these will inspire people to rediscover and more deeply connect with the healing modalities of their people. A core belief of this theory is that deep healing for soul wounds lies somewhere inside of the individual, waiting to be awakened by the songs of one's Grandmothers and the wisdom of one's ancestors. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
