367. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-93853-005
T1  - The effect of sound meditation on quality of life among patients with cancer.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Wheeler, Elizabeth K
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Individuals with cancer face a number of difficulties that impact quality of life. Since quality of life is important for healing and living prosperously, engaging in treatments that enrich quality of life is essential for those living with cancer. Mindfulness and meditative practices are ancient practices known to have many positive health benefits. Sound meditation, although somewhat new to Western culture and research, is predicted to have promising health-related benefits. Research on sound meditation is scarce, and studies examining the effects of sound meditation with singing bowls on quality of life is even more rare. This quantitative, uncontrolled, pre-post, one-group designed study investigated the effects of sound meditation with singing bowls on the quality of life among cancer patients. Paired t-tests were used to measure the change in quality of life, emotional distress (anxiety and depression), and state mindfulness pre- and post- sound meditation intervention over four consecutive weeks. Results reveal sound meditation may be a promising and low-risk complementary treatment to those living with cancer. Overall, participants reported significant improvements in quality of life and an increase in state mindfulness, pre to post intervention. No changes were observed in measures of emotional distress (anxiety and depression). The results should be interpreted with discretion due to the study's small and homogeneous sample. The results of this study have implications for oncology supportive care and mindfulness research.  (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
