T1  - Big or little data for magnetic resonance imaging research in psychiatry?
A1  - Talati, Ardesheer
A1  - van Dijk, Milenna T
A1  - Weissman, Myrna M
A2  - Frodl, T., Janowiz, D., Schmaal, L., Tozzi, L., Dobrowolny, H., Stein, D.J., et al., Gratton, C., Nelson, S.M., Gordon, E.M., Hao, X., Talati, A., Shankman, S.A., Liu, J., Kaiser, J., Tenke, C.E., et al., Marek, S., Tervo-Clemmens, B., Calabro, F.J., Montez, D.F., Kay, B.P., et al., Richtel, M.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Comments on the articles by Scott Marek et al.(see record 2022-48407-017) and "Brain-behavior correlations: Two paths toward reliability" by C.Gratton et al. (2022). The implications touched the work of many psychiatric researchers who see MRI as a tool to finally understand brain function and mechanisms of neuropsychiatric disorders. Indeed, as Marek et al. note, "small-sample neuroimaging will always be critical for studying the human brain," citing longitudinal (within-individual) studies, and studies of rare outcomes as illustrations. Gratton et al. also reiterated Marek et al.'s main message and the value of smaller neuroscience studies that can maximize effect sizes and increase signal to noise. To conclude, Marek et al. and Gratton et al. address the importance of large samples for brain-wide association studies while reiterating the continuing role of smaller-scale research. We agree and add to this discussion the need to carefully calibrate clinical phenotypes when studying psychiatric disease. Deep phenotyping has traditionally been a strength of smaller studies, but working across designs to test reproducibility and generalizability, and the building of large data consortia through the assembly of smaller, well-characterized cohorts, can bridge some of the gaps between big and little data. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
