388. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-85469-150
T1  - The power of the mic: How black women's rap music is shaping sexuality, pleasure, and power for black women.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Lewis, Andrea Aliece
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This study investigates the role of lyrics by Black women rappers (BWRs) in shaping Black women's sexual self-concept and economic empowerment. While previous discourse framed BWRs' lyrics as perpetuating racial and gendered sexual stereotypes such as hypersexuality and promiscuity, recent scholarship posits that these lyrics can also challenge dominant narratives and norms. However, limited research has examined how Black women's perception of Black women's rap lyrics shapes their sexual self-concept and economic empowerment. This qualitative study, building on Black feminist scholarship, utilizes the concept of race-based sexual stereotypes (RBSS) to understand how Black women's interpretations of BWRs' lyrics impact their sexual self-concept and economic empowerment. Moreover, the study employs the theoretical frameworks of hip-hop feminism and sexual script theory to investigate the relationship between Black women's lives with hip-hop and rap culture. The overarching research question is: What role, if any, does Black women's rap music play in how Black women understand and utilize their sexuality? The following sub-questions are addressed: (a) What are Black women's perceptions of how BWRs present or represent themselves sexually in their lyrics? (b) To what extent and in what ways, if any, does Black women's rap music shape Black women's sense of sexual self? (c) To what extent and in what ways, if any, does Black women's rap music shape Black women's economic empowerment? Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 20 self-identified Black women between 18 and 45 who frequently listen to Black women's rap music. Thematic analysis identified the following key themes: (a) the influence of BWRs' music on Black women's conceptualizations of sexual pleasure, (b) Black women's perception of how BWRs manage cultural stereotypes surrounding Black women's sexuality, and (c) Black women's perception of BWRs' messages of the economic power found in intimate relationships. Discourse analysis was employed to situate these themes in within societal power dynamics and embedded meanings. This study illuminates how Black women renegotiate and redefine their sexual identity and economic empowerment in relation to BWRs' music, advancing human sexuality, ethnic studies, women and gender studies, and cultural studies. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
