398. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-85468-253
T1  - The promise of critical youth work in system reform: A mixed-methods investigation of youthworker meaning-making and perceptions of youth-adult partnerships in youth residential facilities at the welfare-justice intersection.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Nalani, Andrew
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Positive youth development frames underlie a wide array of system reform initiatives to mark shift from punitive goals to those that promote system-involved youth's wellbeing, safety, and voice within U.S. child-welfare and juvenile legal systems. However, more than simply reframing system practices, promoting positive development in such systems requires second-order change. One innovative conceptual and practice model, known as youth-adult partnerships (Y-APs), may be a promising approach to promoting positive youth development and serving as a catalyst for second-order change in a system rooted in racialized social control. Although Y-APs have been linked to a wide range of positive youth and community outcomes, their feasibility in spaces of racialized social control, such as youth residential facilities, remains unknown. In this mixed-methods dissertation (QUAL --> Quan), I examine Y-AP feasibility in youth residential facilities at the welfare-justice intersection through direct-line youthworkers' perspectives. In study 1 (qualitative), I use narrative thematic inquiry and counter storytelling to examine workers' narratives of efforts to engage youth and feasibility of shared power practices in these facilities (N= 21). Workers' narratives depict both practices that share similar characteristics to Y-APs (enhanced shared-power practices) and those that are more indicative of status-quo social control (diminished shared-power practices). Explanations for diminished shared power practices fall in two categories: person-centered and structural explanations. Importantly, drawing on theory of racialized organizations, workers' narratives suggest that efforts to enhance shared power practices among staff and youth must also contend with the ideological (racist) and organizational forces that limit staff's own power. In study 2 (quantitative), I use findings from study 1 to develop and examine the criterion validity of a new measure of worker-perceived partnership functioning in congregate care (welfare) and juvenile (justice) facilities (N=76). The measure exhibited the hypothesized two-factor structure, power-with practices and power-over practices, with acceptable-good internal consistency reliability. Criterion validity for the power-with practices subscale was stronger than for the power-over subscale, with Item Response Theory analyses suggesting that this measure was particularly difficult with low discrimination. Results indicated preliminary support for using the new measure to tap a setting-level construct. Recommendations for future scale improvement are discussed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
