505. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-34191-000
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.4324/9781003119593
T1  - The psychological effects of immigrating: A depth psychology perspective on relocating to a new place.
T2  - The psychological effects of immigrating: A depth psychology perspective on relocating to a new place.
A1  - Tyminski, Robert
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - One of the most compelling things about this book is the way in which the author takes us on an impressionistic circumambulation around recurring themes. The book is built upon not only his clinical experiences with refugees and immigrants but also his own family's stories of immigration. Throughout the book he reaches many tentative conclusions about which he is not afraid to remain tentative. And he raises as many questions as he tries to answer. The author is always the keenly sensitive participant witness of himself and others as he goes in search of the many different faces of the psychology of immigrants and immigration. Exploring immigration from historical, clinical, and mythical perspectives, this book considers the varied and complex answers to questions of why people immigrate to entirely new places and leave behind their familiar surroundings and culture. Using research reviews, extensive case material, and literary examples, the author's work will deepen readers' understanding of what is both unique and universal about migratory experiences. The author addresses the negative consequences of xenophobia, the acculturation experiences of children compared to adults, the trauma and psychological issues that arise when seeking refuge or relocating to a new country, and the more recent implications of COVID-19 upon border crossings. The author also re-evaluates the term identity as a psychological shorthand, suggesting that it can flatten the understanding of human complexity and erase migrant and refugee life stories and differences. As one of few books to investigate immigration from a Jungian-oriented perspective, the author's work offers a new and broad perspective on the mental health issues related to immigration. This book will prove essential for clinicians working with refugees and migrants, when in training and in practice, as well as students and practitioners of psychoanalysis seeking to deepen their understanding of migratory experiences. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
