512. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-58330-000
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190648107.001.0001
T1  - Brave new workplace: Designing productive, healthy, and safe organizations.
T2  - Brave new workplace: Designing productive, healthy, and safe organizations.
A1  - Barling, Julian
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This book presents an optimistic picture of work that is productive, healthy, and safe. It takes the reader down a very different path that is consistent with the core tenets of primary prevention initiatives. The core goal is to give people access to the kind of workplaces and working conditions that will help them thrive and succeed, and prevent work-related stress and suffering from emerging in the first place. Organizations do not need to implement all seven characteristics (high-quality leadership, autonomy, belonging, fairness, growth and development, meaningful work, and safe work) to change the way people view their work environments. A fundamental assumption of this approach is that small changes are enough to create productive, healthy, and safe workplaces. The book starts with leadership, but not because leadership is the most effective of all the characteristics leading to productive, healthy, and safe work. Autonomy, belonging, fairness, growth, meaningful work, and safety all follow in alphabetical order, again to emphasize that each of the seven characteristics has an equal place in the model. To enhance the consistency and readability of the book, the chapters largely follow the same sequence. The book examines the attitudinal, performance, and well-being benefits that emerge when organizations get it right. It follows with an evaluation of what happens to work attitudes, work performance, and employee well-being when organizations get it wrong. The book considers how the work characteristic exerts their positive or negative effects. Large bodies of research show how gender and culture influence each of the seven work characteristics, and it examines the role of gender and culture in each chapter. The book illustrates that small changes make a big difference in the long term-perhaps especially during the most trying times-and that effective, evidenced-based interventions are needed to achieve productive, healthy, and safe, work. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
