520. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-38657-059
T1  - Investigating adult ADHD and migraine within an online community.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Baer, Timothy Joseph
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This study investigates a possible association between Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) and migraine headaches and migraine related conditions. This study utilizes an adult sample as few studies have explored this association among populations over the age of 18 years. The current investigation also examines variables with potential clinical significance within the context of an ADHD-migraine association. Salem and colleagues examined empirical research pertinent to detection of an ADHD-migraine association, the results of which were published in 2017. Authors of the study reported finding a positive association between ADHD and migraine headaches via meta-analysis of extant research. However, data used in the analysis derived almost exclusively from studies utilizing child and adolescent samples. Only two studies with adult samples have been conducted since then. The current study aims to address the limited availability of data by examining an ADHD-migraine association utilizing an adult sample. This may contribute to understanding of the generalizability of an ADHD-migraine association across the lifespan. The study also examines factors that could be clinically significant in the context of an ADHD and migraine comorbidity, such as medication overuse. This study examined the frequency of ADHD and migraine headaches, chronic migraine, and probable medication overuse headaches among a sample of adults (18 years and older). Participants were recruited via the migraine headache form (r/migraine) within the social media platform Reddit. Consent procedures and data collection were completed via SurveyMonkey. Participants were classified based on ADHD, migraine headaches, chronic migraine, and probable medication overuse headache screener results. Microsoft Excel and Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS) were utilized to perform all data analysis. Data from 459 participants was analyzed. No significant differences in the proportion of individuals with ADHD were found between groups classified according to either migraine headaches, chronic migraine headaches, or probable medication overuse headache. The frequency of probable medication overuse headache was higher among individuals with ADHD, but this difference was not statistically significant. Clinical characteristics of the study sample revealed high headache comorbidity, rates of ADHD diagnosis, and medical consultation for headaches. A notable discrepancy was found between reported medication overuse headaches and probable medication overuse headache screening results. The study failed to find evidence of a significant association between ADHD and migraine headaches. Clinical characteristics of the study sample and other study limitations may have contributed to this outcome. While lacking statistical significance, results regarding probable medication overuse headache and ADHD may warrant future investigation given the potential clinical implications. Study population presents with clinical characteristics suggesting sample may reflect a clinical sample versus general population. Discrepant findings between self-reported medication overuse headache and screener results offer a potential treatment need with clinical and health care utilization consequences. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
