T1  - Examining organizational embeddedness of full-time, part-time, and contract workers within the values of job satisfaction, pay, supervisory relationships, and promotion.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Ambrose, Montgomery Kronos TimeMaster
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Pay, promotion, job advancement, and supervisory relationships all contribute to employee perception of employee engagement and embeddedness and positive business outcomes. Past examination of business success has been performed with quality standards, organizational culture, and performance metrics, current research has not fully addressed job classification as a mediating influence on employee engagement and embeddedness. Evaluating pay, promotion, job satisfaction, and supervisory relationships and their direct impact on processes used to establish employee engagement and embeddedness will enable organizations to address specific actions to sustain employee engagement and embeddedness and establish strategies to ensure organizational success. A qualitative research study was initiated to measure and assess 137 research subjects in a descriptive study in the United States to understand the relationship of job classification and the four regarding employee engagement and embeddedness. Research subjects were randomly chosen through a simple sampling process using Survey Monkey. The data collection instrument consisted of a series of questions designed to elicit respondents' perception of personal engagement and embeddedness into the corporate structures that employed them. These data collected were analyzed by comparing the total number of each job classification's responses to the other job classification to measure what percentage of each job classification identified positive responses within the survey when compared to the other job classifications researched. Social Exchange Theory offers support towards creating fair and equitable working conditions; however, Social Exchange Theory falls short of understanding recognition, approval, and support of labor capital. Organizational Support Theory details some comprehension into innovative work behaviors, learning goal orientation, and organizational commitment that might offer a more concise understanding of employee engagement and embeddedness and labor capital. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
