A1  - Skinner-Osei, Precious
AI  - Skinner-Osei, Precious; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2945-560X
A2  - Binswanger, I. A., Nowels, C., Corsi, K. F., Glanz, J., Long, J., Booth, R. E., Steiner, J. F., Call, J., Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), Chammah, M., Meagher, T., Dall, C., Department of Health and Human Services, Frings, D., Herring, T., Hooks, G., Sawyer, W., John Hopkins University Coronavirus Resource Center, Kumar, R, LaCourse, A., Listwan, S. J., Reid, S., Hartman, J. L., Lazarus, R. S., Folkman, S., LeMasters, K., Brinkley-Rubenstein, L., Maner, M., Peterson, M., Nowotny, K., Bailey, Z., Marquez, N., Ward, J. A., Parish, K., Maruschak, L. M., Bronson, J., Alper, M., Merelli, A., Moore, K. E., Siebert, S., Brown, G., Felton, J., Johnson, J. E., National Association of Social Workers, Nellis, A., Phillips, L. A., Lindsay, M., Quandt, K. R., Jones, A., Saloner, B., Parish, K., Ward, J. A., DiLaura, G., Dolovich, S., Sawyer, W., Sawyer, W., Wagner, P., Skinner-Osei, P., Stepteau-Watson, D., Soucheray, S., Subbarao, K., Mahanty, S., Sullivan, B., Shapiro, A., Tenny, S., Brannan, J. M., Brannan, G. D., The Council of State Governments Justice Center, The COVID Prison Project, Trotter Ii, R. T., Camplain, R., Eaves, E. R., Fofanov, V. Y., Dmitrieva, N. O., Hepp, C. M., Warren, M., Barrios, B. A., Pagel, N., Mayer, A., Baldwin, J. A., Vasquez-Reyes, M., Wagner, P., Wagner, P., Western, B., Braga, A. A., Davis, J., Sirois, C., World Health Organization, Yancy, C.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This study aimed to explore the impact of COVID-19 on justice-involved African Americans during incarceration and post-release. The study population comprised 25 African American men and women recently released from prison. A qualitative phenomenological approach was employed, and the reflexive thematic analysis process was utilized. In addition, semi-structured interviews were conducted and imported into NVivo 11 qualitative research software. The analysis revealed four major themes: stress, reentry, substance use, and community adjustment. The findings implied a need for (1) policy creation and revision to prepare jails and prisons for public health crises; (2) more realistic post-release coping strategies to prepare individuals to face post-release obstacles, such as COVID-19, housing, employment, relapse, and social reintegration; (3) improvements in data collection and transparency in disseminating such data to enhance healthcare services in jails and prisons; and (4) additional evidence-based research to address current concerns with data availability, misreporting, reporting delays, testing rate differences, and inconsistencies in population estimates. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
