Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Historically, eating disorders (ED) have been perceived as pathologies typical of developed economies. Over the years, EDs have been detected in diverse cultures and developing economies, and rates are increasing worldwide. There are many misconceptions and stereotypes about Latinos and the history and presence of Latinos in the world, including the difference between Latinos and Hispanics. The topics covered in this special issue include instrument validations, preventive interventions, mechanisms that intervene in ED symptoms, treatment modalities, and network analysis. Open science paradigm was also present in this number. Science is about exposing diverse results when they come from the existing reality. Finding mixed and even different results from existing theories and evidence contributes to expanding the frontiers of knowledge. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
