615. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-55661-077
T1  - A path to collaborative decision making through the effective use of dialogic communication and data.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Darwish, Reem
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - The contemporary organization is complex and challenged with adapting to various conditions that are unpredictable and quickly emerging. Conventional wisdom suggests that organizational performance is dependent on the leaders' abilities to efficiently and effectively make decisions concerning how the organization's resources are used to achieve its mission, vision, and strategy (Poyhonen et al., 2019). Ineffective decision making can set off an operational chain reaction that can impact delivery of products and services to customers. The success or failure of an organization depends upon managerial decisions (Akhtar et al., 2014) as well as by the degree of employees' organizational citizenship behavior, defined as constructive employee actions and behaviors that go beyond their formal job descriptions (Odek, 2018). This study examined the impacts of developing skills in the practice of dialogue on decision-making processes within an organization.An intact team of 10 individuals were recruited to participate in the study. Participants engaged in two dialogue training sessions designed to enhance the team's use of data during tactical decision making. Participants completed a pre- and post-survey regarding their decision-making process and discussed their reflections on the training and its impacts in a focus group interview format. Findings were reported related to the researcher's observations and thematic findings from the decision-making workshop, survey results, and focus group interview results. Study data indicated that the participants were initially provoked and excited by the introduction of a new tool and concept. Participants identified needs for more time, empowerment, and data to support a dialogue-based tactical decision-making process. Based on these findings, organizational leaders are encouraged to (a) share more data with team members and encourage them to ask for more data, (b) allow as much time as possible for team members to review data and consider possible solutions, (c) set the expectation and implement frameworks and accountability to encourage active participation and engagement from all team members during decision making, and (d) provide training on the methods and importance of collaborative dialogue. Organizational development practitioners can support these shifts in practice by delivering training and workshops on dialogue, dialogue-based tactical decision making, and tools and frameworks that support dialogue-based data-driven decision making. To further enhance the practice of this type of tactical decision making in organizations, additional research on the impact of leadership styles and behaviors as well as team member personalities on dialogue-based decision making would be beneficial.  (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
