A2  - al MukhainiAM, al HouqaniFA, al KindiRM, AliAM, Al-AmerR, AtoutM, et al., AmendolaS, CeruttiR, PresaghiF, et al., American Psychiatric Association, BenarousX, MoralesP, MayerH, IancuC, EdelY, CohenD, BowkerJC, BowkerMH, SantoJB, OjoAA, EtkinRG, RajaR, CernigliaL, ZorattoF, CiminoS, LaviolaG, AmmanitiM, AdrianiW, ChanHY, LoTW, De LucaM, ChenivesseP., de MicheleF, CareddaM, Delle ChiaieR, SalviatiM, BiondiM., FirthJA, TorousJ, FirthJ, FrankovaI., GavinJ, BrosnanM, GriffithsMD, HamasakiY, Pionnie-DaxN, DorardG, TajanN, HikidaT., HikikomoriST, HoRC, ZhangMW, TsangTY, et al., HuX, FanD, ShaoY., KatoTA, KanbaS, TeoAR, KatoTA, KanbaS, Teo  AlanR, KatoTA, ShinfukuN, TatenoM, KatoTA, TatenoM, ShinfukuN, et al., KatsukiR, TatenoM, KuboH, et al., KondoN, SakaiM, KurodaY, KiyotaY, KitabataY, KurosawaM, KoyamaA, MiyakeY, KawakamiN, TsuchiyaM, TachimoriH, TakeshimaT, KriegA, DickieJR, KuboH, KatsukiR, HorieK, et al., KwonM, KimDJ, ChoH, YangS, LamLT, PengZW, LeeYS, LeeJY, ChoiTY, ChoiJT, LiTMH, WongPWC, LiuLL, LiTMH, TeoAR, KatoTA, WongPWC, MotaAM, LazaroM, GasparE, RosaD, FariasAC, PageMJ, McKenzieJE, BossuytPM, et al., ParkS, JeonHJ, BaeJN, SeongSJ, HongJP, PontesHM, GriffithsMD, PoonLYJ, TsangHWH, ChanTYJ, et al., PozzaA, ColucciaA, KatoT, GaetaniM, FerrettiF., QinL, ChengL, HuM, et al., RozaTH, SpritzerDT, LovatoLM, PassosIC, SamahaAA, FawazM, el YahfoufiN, et al., SilicA, VukojevicJ, CuloI, FalakH., SouilemA, MradA, BrahimT, HannachiR, MechriA, StarcevicV., StavropoulosV, AndersonEE, BeardC, LatifiMQ, KussD, GriffithsM, StipE, ThibaultA, Beauchamp-ChatelA, KiselyS, TatenoM, TeoAR, UkaiW, et al., TeoAR, ChenJI, KuboH, et al., TeoAR, FettersMD, StufflebamK, et al., TeoAR, GawAC, TeoAR, NelsonS, StrangeW, et al., TeoAR, StufflebamK, SahaS, et al., UchidaY, NorasakkunkitV., WongPWC, LiTM, ChanM, et al., WoodRTA, World Health Organization, WuTY, LinCY, ArestedtK, GriffithsMD, BrostromA, PakpourAH, YasumaN, WatanabeK, NishiD, et al., YongRK, FujitaK, ChauPY, SasakiH, YoungKS
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Hikikomori syndrome (HS) is a voluntary prolonged social isolation associated with personal and community impact. Previous evidence pointed out a possible relationship between this syndrome with addiction to digital technologies. Here we aim to understand the relationship between HS and digital technology use, overuse, and addictive behaviors, as well as potential therapeutic approaches. We conducted a systematic review of observational and intervention studies available in PubMed/MEDLINE, LILACS, IBECS, Embase, PsycINFO, and SciELO databases, following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Review and Meta-Analyses Protocols (PRISMA 2020) criteria. The risk of bias was assessed with the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) and Consensus-based Clinical Case Reporting Guideline Development (CARE). Eligibility criteria were pre-, at-risk populations, or those with HS diagnosis, and any kind of technological overuse. Seventeen studies were included in the review, of which eight were cross-sectional, eight were case reports, and one was quasi-experimental. Hikikomori syndrome was associated with addition to digital technologies; no cultural differences were found. Environmental factors, such as a history of bullying, low self-esteem, and grief were identified as precursors of addictive behaviors. The included articles addressed addiction to digital technologies, electronic games, and social networks, among others, in HS. HS is cross-culturally associated with such addiction. The management of these patients remains challenging, and no target evidence-based treatments have been developed. The studies included in this review had several limitations, and more studies with a higher level of evidence are needed to support the results. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
