649. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-46640-036
T1  - Teacher perceptions on the impacts of interdisciplinary studies focused in critical thinking strategies of gifted and talented middle school students.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Norins, Natasha
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Interdisciplinary instruction has an innate place in middle school education today, while this is not a new form of pedagogy. As a result of many talent identification programs closing due to budget restrictions and quality staffing, educators are now looking for innovative ways to keep gifted students engaged in critical thinking and higher-level instruction (Heller, 1993). The research posed questions on how to effectively implement interdisciplinary teaching within the work of a team of teachers to enhance critical thinking in students and increase reflection as a way to collect data from students who experienced these instructional changes. Developing a framework that can be utilized within a general classroom can enhance teacher best practices, as shown in this research, while developing 21st Century Competencies for middle school gifted students. In this dissertation, the research has uncovered teacher perceptions of the need to create units of study that enrich students' daily middle school experience. The culture of the research site took on a whole new culture for enhancing student learning while helping teachers create units of study that crossed disciplines. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
