Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Research on culture and social development has focused mostly on the prevalence of children's social behaviors in different societies. Culture may affect the display of behaviors as well as their implications for social interactions and developmental significance. Culture may facilitate or suppress the development of social behaviors. Culture may also affect the structural and functional features of social relationships, such as friendships and peer groups. The impact of culture often occurs through the social-evaluative processes in which adults and peers perceive and respond to children's specific behaviors in socialization settings according to cultural norms and values in the society. This chapter focuses on children's socioemotional development from a cultural perspective. It discusses theoretical and methodological issues in cultural research, including issues in exploring cultural meanings of children's social behaviors. The chapter reviews the literature on the involvement of culture in the development of socioemotional characteristics in early childhood. It discusses the roles of culture and its change in shaping children's social behaviors. Finally, it also reviews peer relationships and groups in cultural contexts. The chapter concludes with suggestions for future research. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
