T1  - Culture-centered approaches to rhetorical research: Considering domestic violence as a site for intersectional interventions.
T2  - Strategic interventions in mental health rhetoric.
A1  - DeTora, Lisa
A1  - Robinson, Tomeka
A2  - Abramson, A., Andersen, M. L., Collins, P. H., Appleby, G. A., Colon, E., Hamilton, J., Collins, P. H., Collins, P. H., Crenshaw, K. W., Crenshaw, K. W., DeTora, L., Marcelline Block, Angela Laflen, Dutta, M. J., Dutta, M. J., Dutta, M. J., Dutton, D. G., Golant, S. K., Feinberg, C., Lane E.Volpe, Gelles, R., Maynard, P., Herman, J., Holladay, D., Kempe, C. H., Silverman, F. N., Steele, B. F., Droegemueller, W., Silver, H. K., Lockhart, L. L., Danis, F. S., Loseke, D. R., Gelles, R. J., Cavanaugh, M. M., MacKinnon, C., Nelson, H. L., Rita Charon, Martha Montello, Reynolds, J. F., Sokoloff, N. J., Spieldenner, A., Robinson, T. M., Woodruffe, A., Heather Harris, Stone, A., Mallin, A., Gutman, M., Stormer, N., Stormer, N., Sumter, M., Taub, A., Ture, K., Hamilton, C. V., Walker, L.
A3  - Meloncon, Lisa [Ed], Molloy, Cathryn [Ed]
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - This chapter opens with the idea that increased attention to domestic violence is needed in rhetoric of health and medicine work generally and in mental health rhetoric research. It reviews several problematic circumstances around the discourse of domestic violence, including its conflation of mental and physical pathology in battered women, conflicting uses of the same term in different settings, and a reliance on specific and unhelpful heterosexually defined gender and family norms. It also examines several approaches to considering, engaging with, or analyzing the discourses of domestic violence: culture-centered communication, intersectionality, narrative bioethics, rhetorical articulation and recursivity. The chapter suggests that effective rhetorical interventions into domestic violence must be intersectional and culture-centered and ought to account for the problems of recursivity identified, as the stereotypical figure of domestic violence. It suggests that a recursive approach to narrative-building that accounts for culture, social structures, and agency offers a promising model for ensuring that the interventions account for lived reality and do not revert to unhelpfully stereotypical thinking. The chapter offers heuristics that can be easily adapted to various healthcare, mental health, and social situations as well as rhetorical research and fosters increased attention to intersectional and cultural identities in future work. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
