725. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-21924-001
T1  - Servant first, then supervisor: Addiction counseling clinical supervisors practicing servant leadership with their supervisees.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Foss, Nicolas
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - The purpose of this qualitative descriptive study was to explore how addiction counseling clinical supervisors describe the influence of the servant leadership techniques of emotional healing, wisdom, and organizational stewardship on the work engagement of supervisees they lead in addiction treatment agencies in Iowa. The theoretical model utilized in this study was servant leadership theory, with a conceptual framework of work engagement. The research questions were how do addiction counseling clinical supervisors describe the influence of the servant leadership technique of emotional healing (question 1), organizational stewardship (question 2), and wisdom (question 3) on the work engagement of their supervisees. Purposive sampling was utilized to select a sample comprised of 20 addiction counseling clinical supervisors. Data were collected through 15 semi-structured interviews, and one focus group. Thematic analysis was employed as the qualitative data analysis approach to identify six themes related to positive supervision practices, caring for supervisee professional and emotional needs, creation of a supportive work environment, investment of time and energy into the organization, managing change processes, and shared leadership practices. The findings of this study provide support for the use of servant leadership skills in the addiction counseling clinical supervision setting to positively influence the work engagement of supervisees. Recommendations for future leadership research and practice in clinical supervision settings are presented. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
