A2  - Baldermann, J.C., Schuller, T., Kohl, S., Voon, V., Li, N., Hollunder, B., et al., Barron, D.S., Baker, J.T., Budde, K.S., Bzdok, D., Eickhoff, S.B., Friston, K.J., et al., Cole, E.J., Phillips, A.L., Bentzley, B.S., Stimpson, K.H., Nejad, R., Barmak, F., et al., Etkin, A., Joutsa, J., Moussawi, K., Siddiqi, S.H., Abdolahi, A., Drew, W., Cohen, A.L., Marek, S., Tervo-Clemmens, B., Calabro, F.J., Montez, D.F., Kay, B.P., Hatoum, A.S., et al., Meng, Y., Hynynen, K., Lipsman, N., Siddiqi, S.H., Kording, K.P., Parvizi, J., Fox, M.D., Siddiqi, S.H., Schaper, F., Horn, A., Hsu, J., Padmanabhan, J., Brodtmann, A., et al., Siddiqi, S.H., Weigand, A., Pascual-Leone, A., Fox, M.D.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This article discusses the application of human brain connectomics to clinical practice in psychiatry. Unfortunately, despite decades of valiant efforts, connectomics has yet to broadly impact clinical practice in psychiatry. There are at least 2 primary reasons for this shortcoming. First, it is challenging to adequately power neuroimaging studies to detect brain-behavior relationships. Many early-stage studies may have used inadequate sample sizes, leading to false positive findings. Recent studies show how larger sample sizes improve reproducibility, a prerequisite for clinical use. Second, it is challenging to reliably study connectivity measures at the individual level. Repeatedly scanning the same patient under similar conditions does not necessarily yield similar results, highlighting the need to improve test-retest reliability. Recent studies have focused on optimizing scan protocols and analyses to improve reliability at the individual level. The future potential of biomarker development depends on the success of these larger studies with improved methods. When searching for treatment targets, one can draw inspiration from prior successes in neuromodulation. However, it is often impractical to directly compare all possible lesions or stimulation sites, which can come in various shapes, sizes, and locations. This challenge can be overcome by using a human connectome database as a wiring diagram of the brain. There may be an emerging need for subspecialized training for psychiatrists to learn about the use of connectomics to monitor and modulate neurobehavioral systems. In the short term, it may be adequate to follow the literature and attend continuing education events. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
