744. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-21922-285
T1  - A daily investigation of the antecedents and consequences of state authenticity at work.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Yang, Guo
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Authenticity is commonly thought to be beneficial for individuals. However, research on this phenomenon, especially in the workplace, is still incomplete and fragmented. Much of the extant literature has regarded authenticity as a trait but failed to consider it as a dynamic state that can fluctuate across time. Moreover, while some studies have identified a few outcomes of employee authenticity (e.g., job satisfaction, work engagement), research has yet to investigate its antecedents as well as its effects on a broader domain of outcomes with a dynamic perspective. In this dissertation, I integrate self-determination theory with state authenticity as fit to environment theory to explicate how employees' emotional labor strategies (i.e., surface acting, deep acting, genuine emotional display) and work role identity interact to shape state authenticity. More importantly, I examine how employees' state authenticity can influence their task performance, creativity, proactive work behaviors, and voluntary work behaviors on a daily level. Multilevel analyses of 1,226 daily responses from 164 employees showed that surface acting negatively predicts state authenticity, whereas genuine emotional display positively predicts it. It should be noted that work role identity was found to attenuate the positive relationship between genuine emotional display and state authenticity. Moreover, state authenticity was found to enhance employees' task performance, creativity, proactive behaviors (i.e., task proactivity, voice), and positive voluntary behaviors (i.e., OCB), but to diminish their negative voluntary behaviors (i.e., CWB) through the mechanisms of harmonious passion, psychological empowerment, and sense of self-integrity, respectively. This research and the results described in the dissertation aim to provide a more comprehensive understanding of state authenticity in the workplace. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
