T1  - Underestimating harm in mindfulness-based stress reduction.
A1  - Van Dam, Nicholas T
A1  - Galante, Julieta
AI  - Van Dam, Nicholas T.; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1131-0739
AI  - Galante, Julieta; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4108-5341
A2  - Cebolla, A., Demarzo, M., Martins, P., Soler, J., Garcia-Campayo, J., Cebolla, A., Demarzo, M., Martins, P., Soler, J., Garcia-Campayo, J., Farias, M., Maraldi, E., Wallenkampf, K. C., Lucchetti, G., Furukawa, T. A., Noma, H., Caldwell, D. M., Honyashiki, M., Shinohara, K., Imai, H., Chen, P., Hunot, V., Churchill, R., Gold, S. M., Enck, P., Hasselmann, H., Friede, T., Hegerl, U., Mohr, D. C., Otte, C., Goldberg, S. B., Tucker, R. P., Goldberg, S. B., Wielgosz, J., Dahl, C., Schuyler, B., MacCoon, D. S., Rosenkranz, M., Lutz, A., Sebranek, C. A., Davidson, R. J., Hirshberg, M. J., Goldberg, S. B., Rosenkranz, M., Davidson, R. J., Hrobjartsson, A., Emanuelsson, F., Skou Thomsen, A. S., Hilden, J., Brorson, S., Ioannidis, J. P. A., Linden, M., MacCoon, D. G., Imel, Z. E., Rosenkranz, M. A., Sheftel, J. G., Weng, H. Y., Sullivan, J. C., Bonus, K. A., Stoney, C. M., Salomons, T. V., Davidson, R. J., Lutz, A., Santorelli, S. F., Meleo-Meyer, F., Koerbel, L., Kabat-Zinn, J., Schlosser, M., Sparby, T., Voros, S., Jones, R., Marchant, N. L., Schulz, K. F., Altman, D. G., Moher, D., Schulz, K. F., Grimes, D. A., Sterne, J. A. C., White, I. R., Carlin, J. B., Spratt, M., Royston, P., Kenward, M. G., Wood, A. M., Carpenter, J. R., Tierney, J. F., Vale, C., Riley, R., Smith, C. T., Stewart, L., Clarke, M., Rovers, M.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Comments on an article by M. J. Hirshberg et al. (see record 2020-61770-001). In a recent study of harm following the completion of mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR) programs, the authors used a large observational dataset (n = 2155), alongside the active arm (n = 156) and wait-list arm (n = 118) of several amalgamated randomized controlled trials. The authors interpret their results as 'strong evidence that MBSR is no more harmful than no treatment'. While seemingly conclusive, authors would like to point out some potentially serious limitations to the methods used that undermine confidence in the authors' conclusions. The authors rightly assert that there is no consensus about 'harm' in psychosocial interventions. Acknowledgement of this lack of consensus is not, however, justification to ignore the many ways in which psychosocial interventions may potentially cause harm. The overall level of impairment is significantly higher in the community sample than either of the two groups in the randomized controlled trials and is above the T value commonly used to detect psychological distress. Thus, the community sample represents a large selection of individuals who sought out and paid to receive MBSR, presumably, with the aim of helping them to manage their psychological distress. As this represents a non-blinded intervention, it likely suffers from inflated effect size estimates and attrition rates. The authors mention that they collated data from three trials and give two grant numbers but no trial protocols or publications. Authors were unable to find waitlist-controlled trials published under the grant U01AT002114-01A1. Authors found one waitlist and Health Enhancement Program controlled trial under P01AT004952 that measured GSI; curiously enough they failed to report GSI result. Authors failed to find any information on two of the three trials. The authors use the deterioration of the wait-list group, compared to the improvement of the community group as a means of saying that community MBSR is preventative of harm. The comparison is inappropriate for three reasons. The first is that one cannot reasonably compare an active, open-label intervention to the control arm of an RCT. The second is that the estimation of effect in the community MBSR group is likely an overestimate, as discussed above. The third is the fact that participants allocated to wait-list groups in active randomized controlled trials are known to exhibit the nocebo effect. For all these reasons authors do strongly disagree with the assertion that 'these data provide strong evidence against claims that MBSR may increase harm'. The evidence presented in this article is insufficient to support the claim. Moreover, the authors appeal to a logical fallacy; absence of evidence rather than evidence of absence - not finding evidence of adverse effects in MBSR does not mean that there are none. Authors are also concerned that the abstract, often the only section people read, fails to acknowledge any of the limitations that undermine confidence in the results. The claims made by the authors provide a much sought-after reassurance that is as yet unfounded in our view. A series of recent studies have suggested that adverse events can and do happen in meditation-based interventions including those with a focus on mindfulness. None of this is to say that people should not undertakeMBSR or thatMBSR is likely to be harmful.Much to the contrary,MBSR is likely to be helpful for most people for a wide array of issues. However, methodological and reporting quality needs to improve before making strong evidential claims about adverse effects in interventions with high public health relevance such as MBSR. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
