774. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-21918-233
T1  - The moderating role of absorption capacity on the relationship between employee learning orientation and open innovation performance among SMES in Ghana: A correlational study.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Abdulai, Munkaila
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - The study examined the moderating role of absorption capacity on the relationship between managers learning orientation and open innovation adoption. Few studies on the human side of open innovation (called micro foundations level factors) are found in the literature, and those that do tend to limit the focus on managers' educational attainment, entrepreneurial orientation, and leadership style, as factors influencing open innovation performance. The literature review revealed that there has not been any cited studies on managers' attributes, such as learning orientation which is essential for open innovation adoption. Using SMEs in a developing economy context like Ghana, managers' learning orientation is examined concerning open innovation adoption. The study explored whether there is a relationship between managers learning orientation and open innovation adoption and whether such a relationship may be moderated by the absorption capacity of the firm. This study used a quantitative non-experimental correlational design to fill the research gap. The target population is managers of SMEs in Ghana responsible for innovation related responsibilities with a year minimum of experience in the firm. In line with the G*Power analysis, a sample size of 147 respondents participated in the study. Three validated adapted survey instruments were used for the data collection. The data was analyzed using Spearman and SmartPLS. The results revealed a statistically significant relationship between managers' learning orientation and inbound open innovation practices while having a statistically insignificant relationship with outbound open innovation practices. It was observed that the relationships were not statistically moderated significantly by the absorption capacity of the firm in both cases. Management implications of the findings and some recommendations are presented therein. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
