804. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-10940-032
T1  - Identifying factors that influence women to become and remain police officers.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Layden, Lisa
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - The purpose of this study was to learn about the real life experiences of women in policing. Women bring an invaluable perspective to modern policing. Numerous research studies have documented these benefits (Brandl, Stroshine and Frank, 2001, Paoline and Terrill, 2004, Schuck and Rabe-Hemp, 2005). However, the percentage of women in policing has only increased incrementally over the last century, stagnated during the past two decades, and currently remains at approximately 12 percent (Bureau of Justice Statistics, 2016). The literature indicates gender stereotyping within the field of policing may be the primary reason women are not significantly participating in this career path. Strong gender beliefs and negative stereotypes about women persist in this male-dominated service field among co-workers and supervisors (Block and Anderson, 1974, Bell, 1982, Garcia, 2003, Wilson, 2016). Research about the police culture describes the environment as hypermasculine, where masculine values override feminine ideals and dictate the nature of policing and the roles that are appropriate for women (Waddington, 1999). Currently, police tactics and use of force, particularly lethal force has come under intense scrutiny, and the public is demanding fundamental changes and police reform. Government officials at every level are being called upon to find more effective and more humane ways to serve citizens. Contemporary research has documented several important outcomes related to the level of women in police agencies, including higher levels of communication with the public, lower levels of aggressive tactics and force, lower levels of lethal force, and better public perception (Brandl, et al., 2001). These studies suggest that recruiting and retaining more policewomen may decrease unnecessary force and deadly force incidents and build public trust and respect (Brandl, et al., 2001). Thus, understanding more about the factors that influence women to become police officers and remain in the field is a relevant area of research. I conducted a qualitative study to assess how women police officers give meaning to their experiences and how this influences persistence in the field. My primary goal was to learn about their experiences entering and remaining in the field, their perceptions of policing, and their role in a police agency. My findings indicate gender discrimination is pervasive and amplified in policing. Police culture fosters an environment where masculine traits and ideals are valued, and feminine ideals are not only de-valued, but often become the antithesis. In regard to providing service to the community, my findings indicate women approach policing from a different perspective than their male counterparts, which reflects current research about how women officers influence community relations, and lower levels of aggression and force. In addition, my qualitative data illustrates how the perspective of women officers influences their thought process, actions and final outcomes. Understanding how women officers benefit the community and seeing how agencies can attract and keep women in policing has important policy implications for policing and the community and contributes to the important scholarship on women and policing. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
