T1  - Using the Reproducible Open Coding Kit & Epistemic Network Analysis to model qualitative data.
A1  - Zorgo, Szilvia
A1  - Peters, Gjalt-Jorn
AI  - Peters, Gjalt-Jorn; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0336-9589
A2  - Bowman, D., Swiecki, Z., Cai, Z., Wang, Y., Eagan, B., Linderoth, J., Shaffer, D., Buetow, S., Gherardi, S., Turner, B., Helman, C., Larson, R. B., Morse, J., Barrett, M., Olson, K., Spiers, J., Shaffer, D., Shelton, C. L., Smith, A. F., Mort, M., Siebert-Evenstone, A. L., Arastoopour, G., Collier, W., Swiecki, Z., Ruis, A., Williamson Shaffer, D., Tariq, S., Woodman, J., UNESCO Recommendation on Open Science, Uygun Tunc, D., Tunc, M. N., Lakens, D., Williamson Shaffer, D., Collier, W., Ruis, A., Zorgo, S., Peters, G.-J., Krisztina, C.-V., Jeney, A., Ruis, A., Zorgo, S., Peters, G.-J. Y., Zorgo, S., Purebl, G., Zana, A., Zorgo, S., Swiecki, Z., Ruis, A. R.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background: Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) is a unified, quantitative - qualitative method aiming to draw from both methodological worlds by leveraging a data set containing raw and quantified qualitative data, as well as metadata about data providers or the data itself. ENA generates network models depicting the relative frequencies of co-occurrences for each unique pair of codes in designated segments of qualitative data. Methods: This step-by-step tutorial demonstrates how to model qualitative data with ENA through its quantification via coding and segmentation. Data was curated with the Reproducible Open Coding Kit (ROCK), a human- and machine-readable standard for representing coded qualitative data, enabling researchers to document their workflow, as well as organize their data in a format that is agnostic to software of any kind. Results: ENA allows researchers to obtain insights otherwise unavailable by depicting relative code frequencies and co-occurrence patterns, facilitating a comparison of those patterns between groups and individual data providers. Conclusions: ENA aids reflexivity, moves beyond code frequencies to depict their interactions, allows researchers to easily create post-hoc groupings of data providers for various comparisons, and enables conveying complex results in a visualization that caters to both qualitative and quantitative sensibilities. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
