A2  - Banerjee, R., Movahedazarhouligh, S., Millen, K., Luckner, J. L., Bollen, K., Cacioppo, J. T., Kaplan, R. M., Krosnick, J. A., Olds, J. L., Chalcraft, D. R., Cook, B. G., Collins, L. W., Cook, S. C., Cook, L., Earp, B. D., Trafimow, D., Gall, M. D., Gall, J. P., Borg, W. R., Hope, D., Dewar, A., Hay, C., Huffmeier, J., Mazei, J., Schultze, T., Ioannidis, J. P. A., Makel, M. C., Plucker, J. A., Freeman, J., Lombardi, A., Simonsen, B., Coyne, M., Martin, G. N., Clarke, R. M., Maxwell, S. E., Lau, M. Y., Howard, G. S., Neuliep, J. W., Crandall, R., Nosek, B. A., Alter, G., Banks, G. C., Borsboom, D., Bowman, S. D., Breckler, S. J., Buck, S., Chambers, C. D., Chin, G., Christensen, G., Contestabile, M., Dafoe, A., Eich, E., Freese, J., Glennerster, R., Goroff, D., Green, D. P., Hesse, B., Humphreys, M., Yarkoni, T., ..., Nosek, B. A., Spies, J. R., Motyl, M., Reichow, B., B.Reichow, B. A.Boyd, E. E.Barton, S. L.Odom, Schmidt, S., Simons, D. J.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - This article discusses replication research in education. The education community must aim to conduct, encourage, and support rigorous research that is transparent, actionable, and focused on consequential outcomes. The ultimate goal of an educational scientific endeavor is to provide empirical evidence to improve education practice and policy and share that evidence in a way that can be used by educators, families, policymakers, researchers, and the community. The appraisal of research evidence is integral to establishing an evidence base. Research appraisal can be addressed by replication of intervention studies by different researchers across settings and participants. Replicability, generalizability, and reproducibility have been interchangeably used in the field. Despite the well-recognized importance of replication in this scientific endeavor, replications are under- appreciated, and there is "little reinforcement in the field for conducting replications to affirm or reject the validity of prior evidence and few consequences for getting it wrong". However, the recent analysis of research and practices suggests that the tide is beginning to turn, even if very slowly. It is encouraging to note the recent trends in awareness, acknowledgement, promotion, and support of replication research and its dissemination. Higher education programs, too, will need to demonstrate a culture of replication research by valuing direct and conceptual replication studies and publications in promotion and tenure decisions. he direct and conceptual replication of peerreviewed, published research not only prepares the future leaders in strong research methodology, it also builds on the scientific evidence for practices. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
