A2  - Campbell, D. T., Collins, H. M., Denzin, N. K., Lincoln, Y. S., N. K.Denzin, Y. S.Lincoln, Foote, M. Q., Bartell, T. G., Hellstrom, T., Jacobson, D., Mustafa, N., Lincoln, Y. S., Guba, E. G., Makel, M. C., Hodges, J., Cook, B. G., Plucker, J. A., Makel, M. C., Plucker, J. A., Morrow, S. L., Patton, M. Q., Plucker, J. A., Makel, M. C., Pratt, M. G., Kaplan, S., Whittington, R., Rowe, W. E., D.Coghlan, M.Brydon-Miller, Schmidt, S., Stroebe, W., Strack, F., van Manen, M., Yin, R. K.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - This article discusses the relevance of replication to the qualitative lens. Replication is relevant to the qualitative lens. The extent of its relevance - and in which contexts it is more relevant - needs to be unpacked. But the same is true in quantitative research. When it comes to replication, it may be that the quantitative and qualitative traditions are two countries divided more by language than by values or goals. It may also be that progress in quantitative replication represents quantitative researchers catching up to concerns that qualitative researchers have been grappling with for decades. Similar to transferability, replication in qualitative work can draw on the rich tradition of reflexivity by assessing how differences in positionality are associated with differences in inferences and interpretations. Replications by researchers from different positionalities have the potential to propel the field towards even more intentional and transparent research. Replication can promote the importance of contextual exploration as well as provide opportunities for triangulation by researchers with different positionalities. Some have argued that replication "missed the point" of qualitative research. Given the prevalence of qualitative research in education, it is important to examine replication's relevance. In this commentary, we argue that replication is relevant to the qualitative lens in at least three ways. First, replication supports the established values in qualitative research of transparency and intentionality. Second, replication can be used to assess the well-established tradition of transferability. Third, replication can evaluate connections between reflexivity, as evidenced by positionality statements, and qualitative research findings. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
