T1  - How should educational research respond to the replication "crisis" in the social sciences? Reflections on the papers in the special issue.
T2  - Special Issue: Replication study in education
A1  - Wiliam, Dylan
A2  - Bloom, H. S., Hill, C. J., Black, A. R., Lipsey, M. W., Bokhove, C., Bonferroni, C. E., Borges, J. L., D. A.Yates, Brewer, J. K., Brown, J. F., Bryan, C. J., Tipton, E., Yeager, D. S., Cartwright, N., Cohen, J., Dunn, O. J., Foung, D., Kohnke, L., Gelman, A., Loken, E., Goldacre, B., Drysdale, H., Marston, C., Mahtani, K. R., Dale, A., Milosevic, I., Slade, E., Hartley, P., Heneghan, C., Hume, D., P.Nidditch, Joyce, K. E., Kerr, N. L., Levin, H. M., Belfield, C., Muennig, P., Rouse, C., Morrison, K., Mosteller, F. W., Open Science Collaboration, Perry, T., Morris, R., Lea, R., Scheffe, H., Sedlmeier, P., Gigerenzer, G., See, B. H., Morris, R., Gorard, S., Siddiqui, N., Easterbrook, M. J., Nieuwenhuis, M., Fox, K., Harris, P. R., Banerjee, R., Sisk, V. F., Burgoyne, A. P., Sun, J., Butler, J. L., Macnamara, B. N., Speckesser, S., Runge, J., Foliano, F., Bursnall, M., Hudson-Sharp, N., Rolfe, H., Anders, J., Starkey, P., Klein, A., Clarke, B., Baker, S, Thomas, J., Suarez, N., Gomez, V., Lara, D. G., Tukey, J. W.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - This article discusses the responses made by educational research to the replication "crisis" in the social sciences for the current special issue of Educational Research and Evaluation. There are serious problems in any field, but are exacerbated in the social sciences, where the statistical power of reported studies is generally low. They are particularly salient in education for two additional reasons. The first is that policy-relevant effect sizes can be rather small. The annual increase in achievement for students in elementary and secondary schools, at least as measured by standardized tests, is around 0.4. Many educational interventions are mediated by teachers, who are nested in schools, that are in turn nested in districts, so there is often a considerable degree of clustering in the data. As a result, it is not uncommon to find that well over 100 schools are required for educationally important effects to be demonstrated. What is, perhaps, surprising, is how long it has taken the field of educational research to respond to this issue. Fortunately, in recent years the issue of replicability in educational research has gained greater attention, with organizations such as the Education Endowment Foundation devoting considerable resources to replication studies. Perhaps more importantly, the issue of replication, particularly in education, has broadened its focus considerably. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
