A2  - Altman, D. G., Royston, P., Bahji, A., Vazquez, G. H., Zarate, C. A., Jr., Berman, R. M., Cappiello, A., Anand, A., et al., Correia-Melo, F. S., Leal, G. C., Vieira, F., et al., McInnes, L. A., Qian, J. J., Gargeya, R. S., DeBattista, C., Heifets, B. D., Sakurai, H., Jain, F., Foster, S., et al.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Presents a study which aims to examine the trajectory of depression severity with ketamine and esketamine treatment in a clinical setting. For this comparative analysis, the authors reviewed retrospective data for all Yale IPS patients receiving intravenous ketamine or intranasal esketamine between September 2016 and April 2021. The Yale Institutional Review Board approved this analysis of existing clinical data and waived informed consent per the Common Rule. The analysis followed the ISPOR reporting guideline. Of 210 included patients, 129 (61.4%) received intravenous ketamine and 81 (38.6%) received intranasal esketamine. There were no group differences for intravenous ketamine vs intranasal esketamine in meanuicidal ideation scores on MADRS item vs 2.64 to 1.26 and QIDS-SR item 12 to 0.50 vs 1.23 to 0.45. Subgroup analysis of 46 patients aged 65 years or older showed response and remission rates of 32.6% and 30.4%, respectively, with no group differences. This comparative analysis evaluating the trajectory of depression severity with ketamine and esketamine yielded no significant differences between groups based on the primary outcome measure. There were no significant between-group differences based on available demographic factors. However, the study demographics may not be representative of the general population, suggesting accessibility issues with these treatments. This study's limitations include the nonrandomized nature of treatment allocation and the retrospective nature of the analysis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
