T1  - Millennial attitudes towards sharing mobile phone location data with health agencies: A qualitative study.
A1  - Murphy, Hannah
A1  - Keahey, Lauren
A1  - Bennett, Emma
A1  - Drake, Archie
A1  - Brooks, Samantha K
A1  - Rubin, G. James
A2  - Bhattacharya, D., Ghosh, J. K., Boccardo, P., Samadhiya, N. K., Birkhoff, S. D., Smeltzer, S. C., Braun, V., Clarke, V., Citizens Advice Bureau, Clarke, R., Cuthbertson, A., Deloitte, Direct Marketing Association, Direct Marketing Association, Drake, A., Pollitt, A., Sklar, E., Smith, L., Parsons, S., Schneider, E., Gibson, L. A., Sodeman, W. A., Granville, K., Houses of Parliament, Information Commissioner's Office, International Telecommunication Union, Ipsos MORI, Kalkman, S., van Delden, J., Banerjee, A., Tyl, B., Mostert, M., van Thiel, G., Kratzke, C., Cox, C., Landau, S., McNeill, A., Brose, L. S., Calder, R., Hitchman, S. C., Morgan, D. L., Oliver, N., Matic, A., Frias-Martinez, E. F., Orwell, G., Royal Statistical Society, Rubin, G. J., Webster, R., Rubin, A. N., Amlot, R., Grey, N., Greenberg, N., Sagoe, D., ScienceWise Expert Resource Centre, Singh, K., Drouin, K., Newmark, L. P., Lee, J., Faxvaag, A., Rozenblum, R., Pabo, E. A., Landman, A., Klinger, E., Bates, D. W., The Wellcome Trust, Tizzoni, M., Bajardi, P., Decuyper, A., Kon Kam King, G., Schneider, C. M., Blondel, V., Smoreda, Z., Gonzalez, M. C., Colizza, V., Salathe, M., Wesolowski, A., Stresman, G., Eagle, N., Wiedmer, T.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - The use of mobile phone devices leaves digital traces which include personal data of the user's location and can be used to produce aggregated and anonymised location data. There is widespread interest in utilising different types of mobile phone location data to optimise emergency health responses within a range of settings, however until now there has been no qualitative research exploring public opinions on sharing such data. A qualitative study with 40 millennials (born between 1981-2000) across six focus groups were conducted before and after widespread news reports about the leaking of personal data on social media and a major public health incident in the UK. Thematic analysis was used to identify themes in the data. Analysis identified four main themes and five subthemes suggesting the main concepts related to attitudes and concerns were 'Control', 'Trust', 'Risks to sharing' and 'Pros and cons of data sharing'. Millennials were generally accepting of sharing different types of location data with ambulance services and a public health agency if they were able to give explicit consent and retain some personal control towards how their data would be used. The results suggest that policymakers who wish to use aggregated and anonymized mobile phone location data to improve healthcare services should focus on reassurance about how data will be used and promoting public trust. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
