N2  - The increasing availability of individual participant data (IPD) in the social sciences offers new possibilities to synthesize research evidence across primary studies. Two-stage IPD meta-analysis represents a framework that can utilize these possibilities. While most of the methodological research on two-stage IPD meta-analysis focused on its performance compared with other approaches, dealing with the complexities of the primary and meta-analytic data has received little attention, particularly when IPD are drawn from complex sampling surveys. Complex sampling surveys often feature clustering, stratification, and multistage sampling to obtain nationally or internationally representative data from a target population. Furthermore, IPD from these studies is likely to provide more than one effect size. To address these complexities, we propose a two-stage meta-analytic approach that generates model-based effect sizes in Stage 1 and synthesizes them in Stage 2. We present a sequence of steps, illustrate their implementation, and discuss the methodological decisions and options within. Given its flexibility to deal with the complex nature of the primary and meta-analytic data and its ability to combine multiple IPD sets or IPD with aggregated data, the proposed two-stage approach opens up new analytic possibilities for synthesizing knowledge from complex sampling surveys. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
Impact Statement
Meta-analyses are key tools to synthesize and accumulate evidence in psychological research. The increasing availability of data from individual participants shared by researchers and institutions provides new opportunities for meta-analyses. Some of the openly available data sets are based on complex sampling designs-that is, designs that typically involve multistage sampling (e.g., randomly sampling schools first and then students within schools), clustering (e.g., students nested in schools), and stratification (e.g., randomly sampling students from different socioeconomic status groups). These designs pose methodological challenges to the meta-analysis of individual participant data, not only when generating the effect sizes of interest but also when synthesizing these effect sizes across primary studies or data sets. Specifically, both the analysis and synthesis of complex survey data need to consider the sample design features of the primary studies and the dependencies among effect sizes during the meta-analysis. This article presents a two-stage approach to synthesizing individual participant data from complex sampling designs. We illustrate this approach with two meta-analytic examples from educational psychology (i.e., the negative effect of classroom achievement on students' self-concept in mathematics [big-fish-little-pond-effect] and gender differences in secondary-school students' digital skills) and present hands-on steps. Researchers can find extensive online supplementary material to reproduce our results and translate the syntax to their research contexts. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
