856. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-10931-103
T1  - Relationships among public school general education teachers, who teach students with chronic illnesses, and teacher attrition and teacher mobility.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Grossman Brown, Melissa A
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - School districts and stakeholders contributed to classroom teachers' increasing expectations and responsibilities. Academic diversity continued to grow and expand, including students with disabilities. General education teachers were prepared to teach everyone in the class. They had a basic understanding of the Special Education process, a basic understanding of disabilities, and a basic understanding of Special Education laws. Moreover, the increase in academic diversity and the number of students with disabilities in the general education classroom created additional challenges, expectations, and responsibilities. Researchers noted teacher attrition was on the rise. Minimal extant literature related to general education teachers, teacher attrition, and teacher mobility was available. The purpose of my qualitative, interpretive study was to examine the relationship among public school general education teachers, Kindergarten through 12th grade, who taught chronically ill students, teacher attrition, and teacher mobility in Tennessee. After collecting data from 17 participants, via an online questionnaire, I analyzed the data using open, axial, and selective coding to generate themes. General education teachers in Tennessee indicated they instructed a broad range of students with chronic illnesses daily and supported students with individualized supplemental supports through modifications or accommodations. Additionally, these general education teachers revealed the importance of an individualized support network for their students. Furthermore, the participants acknowledged the physical, emotional, and professional demands of teaching chronically ill students; however, they chose to remain in their current teaching roles. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
