858. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-01890-075
T1  - Organizational culture: A case study within a multinational technology company.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Gray, Adam Joseph
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Corporate culture, like personal character, is an amorphous quality that exerts a powerful influence as it encompasses a common set of behaviors and underlying mindsets and beliefs that shape how employees work and interact day to day. Ultimately, organizational culture is allowing organizations to create and establish a critical foundation of effectiveness that genuinely outlines what employees should be doing along with how they should go about doing it. Poor organizational culture usually has a negative impact on the performance of the organization and reduces the shareholders' return on investment. Furthermore, many leaders do not exhibit excellent leadership skills which negatively affects their organizational culture. SkyRise, a pseudonym for a multinational technology company, recently found six key leaders leaving the organization within a 4-month period. Therefore, it is important for SkyRise to better understand how employees view its organizational culture and how the culture can be improved. The purpose of this study was to understand how employees view its organizational culture and how the culture can be improved. This research was guided by the following research questions: What are the employee perceptions of the values, expectations, and practices that guide the organizational culture of SkyRise? How can those perceptions be incorporated to increase the leaders' awareness of the organizational culture of SkyRise? In this qualitative study, convenience sampling was used to identify 20 different respondents within SkyRise to participate in online questionnaires', interviews and a focus group that contained both open-ended and less structured questions and questionnaires. The study's findings were analyzed using content analysis. Content analysis highlighted the reoccurring themes from the responses of the selected participants along with allowing the findings to be presented descriptively by explaining the discovered trends. The first theme was focus on authority, the second theme was adhering to a well-defined vision and mission and the final theme was employee-focused leadership. The primary findings were that organizational support, clear communication, diversity, and transparency improved organizational culture. In the future, the organization should focus on coaching employees, maintaining close relations, and ensure that the employment process involves competent individuals. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
