859. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-01889-231
T1  - Capacity building in restorative justice: A meta-thematic synthesis of relational moral developmental themes regarding attitudes towards punitiveness.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Ronning, Gina E
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Restorative justice holds great promise for criminal justice reform as a shift away from the harms of a mass incarceration culture. Research statistics have shown that despite lowering crime rates and the effectiveness of rehabilitative approaches to crime, justice policies in general still remain punitive in nature. Tough-on-crime approaches still dominate the criminal justice discourse. Furthermore, as the United States is faced with a reckoning of racial justice, citizens are being called to examine the truth behind the disproportionate impacts the current justice system has on communities of color, particularly Black and Brown men. This begs the question, why do individuals still support punitive responses to crime despite growing evidence that rehabilitative approaches reduce recidivism and increase public safety? Proponents of restorative justice often face challenges in establishing critical buy-in and support for restorative approaches. This dissertation provides a thematic, meta-synthesis exploration into the emerging themes on the subject of punitive justice attitudes over a decade of research. This research employs a grounded theory approach, and specifically explores punitiveness as it may relate to moral reasoning and development. The theoretical constructs guiding a majority of the research on the subject of punitiveness are: Schwart's Value Typology, Haidt's Moral Foundation Theory, Learning Mindsets by Dweck; Social Identity Theory by Tajfel; Social Closeness Theory by Linke, and Racial Resentment Theory by Kinder and Sanders (1996). These constructs are then synthesized and explored alongside of Kohlberg's Theory of Moral Developmental Stages. This study also identifies several distinct themes believed to be underlying motivators for punitive attitudes, and the degree to which these themes influence justice behaviors and beliefs is also explored. The purpose of this research is to help bridge interdisciplinary ideas as a means for generating a more wholistic understanding of moral development. Insights gained from this research may help to serve as a compass for more data-driven restorative justice capacity building approaches and curriculum. Furthermore, the role of transformative learning processes is also discussed as a potential framework for restorative justice education and dialogue processes. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
