861. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-01887-079
T1  - House of brews: College football sales and game day incidents.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Thomas, Celina Bustamante
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - It is well documented that some colleges and universities within the U. S. sell wine and beer in their stadiums on football Saturdays. Alcohol abuse in relation to sporting events is an obvious public health concern and a risk management issue for college athletic departments and their administrators. Because alcohol consumption on college game days is not new, risk management strategies implemented outside the football stadium include limiting alcohol to designated tailgate areas, increasing police presence in tailgate areas and around the stadium, and prohibiting alcohol being brought into the stadium (Glassman et. Al., 2010; Miller, et al., 2019; Hardin et al., 2013). These measures and others within the stadium have been undertaken in the interest of public relations and protecting the university, as well as the campus community at large. But with in-stadium alcohol sales are there other risks to fans? Does the availability of beer and wine inside the stadium lead to more responsible drinking? Because of the availability of alcohol within the stadium, are there fewer drinking-related arrests on game-days? As more schools adopt policies for general alcohol sales at their football stadiums, the answers to these questions become significant. Awareness of legal and risk management implications arising from drinking such as overconsumption, underage consumption, crowd control, and drunk driving must be addressed. This empirical study is designed to answer these questions by sharing data collected from a Big Ten University campus. The purpose of the study was to compare the number of alcohol-related incidents (i.e., public intoxication, illegal consumption, Operating While Intoxicated) one season prior to in-stadium alcohol sales compared to the season when alcohol sales were implemented. Specifically, the study investigates the alcohol sales at Indiana University's football games in 2019 to determine if there was a reduction in alcohol-related incidents documented by two law enforcement agencies compared to the 2018 season. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
