865. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2023-01881-281
T1  - Experiences of Central American fathers acculturating to the United States.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Adent, Yesenia
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - The current study was designed to understand the experience of Central American fathers acculturating in the United States. After completing a review of literature, it was determined that more research was needed to study Latino males independent of females as well as Latinos in other geographical areas. Additionally, there was a need for more qualitative studies as well as studies that focused on Latino subgroups individually, such as Central Americans. Therefore, a generic qualitative inquiry using a thematic analysis approach was completed with a sample of Central American fathers within the metropolitan statistical area. There was a total of 8 participants in the current study. Upon completion of data analysis, there were five themes identified with ten corresponding patterns. The themes of the study were: heritage, family bonding, spirituality, adaptation, and machismo. The heritage theme had two patterns, Spanish language retention and keeping home alive. The family bonding theme had two patterns, perception of United States culture and limited American friends initially. The Spirituality theme had two patterns, managing stress and coping with isolation. The adaptation theme had two patterns, changes in parenting style and embracing the culture. The machismo theme had two patterns, respect for women and an outdated custom. The study results demonstrated that the fathers had successfully integrated in the United States by incorporating their native cultural beliefs along with aspects of United States culture. Practical applications and future research recommendations were discussed in detail in the final chapter. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
