T1  - The economic cost of violence against women and girls in low- and middle-income countries: A systematic review of the evidence.
A1  - Vyas, Seema
A1  - Meinhart, Melissa
A1  - Troy, Katrina
A1  - Brumbaum, Hannah
A1  - Poulton, Catherine
A1  - Stark, Lindsay
AI  - Vyas, Seema; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3066-664X
A2  - Adam, T., Bathija, H., Bishai, D., Bonnenfant, Y., Darwish, M., Huntington, D., Johansen, E., Boothby, N., Stark, L., Bundhamcharoen, K., Odton, P., Mugem, S., Phulkerd, S., Dhisayathikom, K., Brown, D. W., Tangcharoensathien, V., Butchart, A., Brown, D., Khanh-Huynh, A., Corso, P., Florquin, N., Muggah, R., Chan, K. L., Cho, E. Y., Chandan, J. S., Taylor, J., Bradbury-Jones, C., Nirantharakumar, K., Kane, E., Bandyopadhyay, S., Curca, G., Buda, O., Capatina, O., Marinescu, M., Hostiuc, S., Dermengiu, D., Cartina, C., Cretoiu, V. A., Stoica, N. A., Badescu, I., Voinea, M., Darie, C., Apostu, I., Banciu, D. P. G., Balica, E. G., Brezeanu, O., Constantinescu, A., Balan, L., Dalal, K., Dawad, S., Day, T., McKenna, K., Bowlus, A., Dolan, P., Loomes, G., Peasgood, T., Tsuchiya, A., Ekenze, S., Ezegwui, H., Adiri, C., Ellsberg, M., Arcas, C. C., Ellsberg, M., Jansen, H. A. F. M., Heise, L., Watts, C. H., Garcia-Moreno, C., Espinosa, R., Gutierrez, M. I., Mena-Munoz, J. H., Cordoba, P., Ferrari, G., Torres-Rueda, S., Michaels-Igbokwe, C., Watts, C., Jewkes, R., Vassall, A., Fourozan, A., Dejman, M., Baradaran-Eftekhari, M., Bagheri-Yazdi, A., Garcia-Moreno, C., Jansen, H. A. F. M., Ellsberg, M., Heise, L., Watts, C., Gonsalves, G., Kaplan, E., Paltiel, A., Husereau, D., Drummond, M., Petrou, S., Carswell, C., Moher, D., Greenberg, D., Augustovski, F., Briggs, A. H., Mauskopf, J., Loder, E., Inter-Agency Standing Committee, Laisser, R. M., Lugina, H. I., Lindmark, G., Nystrom, L., Emmelin, M., Lince-Deroche, N., Berry, K. M., Hendrickson, C., Sineke, T., Kgowedi, S., Mulongo, M., Matzopoulos, R., Bowman, B., Butchart, A., Mercy, J. A., McCleary-Sills, J., Namy, S., Nyoni, J., Rweyemamu, D., Salvatory, A., Steven, E., Moher, D., Liberati, A., Tetzlaff, J., Altman, D., Morrison, A. R., Orlando, M. B., Raghavendra, S., Duvvury, N., Ashe, S., Roldos, M. I., Corso, P., Seff, I., Stark, L., Tchamo, S., Mucambe, M., Jose, G., Manuel, B., Mataveia, G., United Nations, Vassall, A., Sweeney, S., Kahn, J. G., Gomez, G., Bollinger, L., Marseille, E., Herzel, B., Plosky, W. D., Cunnama, L., Sinanovic, E., Bautista, S., GHCC Technical Advisory Group, Vetten, L., Vyas, S., Vyas, S., Mbwambo, J., Waters, H. R., Hyder, A. A., Rajkotia, Y., Basu, S., Butchart, A., World Bank, World Bank, World Health Organization, World Health Organization, World Health Organization, Yodanis, C. L., Godenzi, A., Stanko, E. A.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Evidence demonstrating the economic burden of violence against women and girls can support policy and advocacy efforts for investment in violence prevention and response programming. We undertook a systematic review of evidence on the costs of violence against women and girls in low- and middle-income countries published since 2005. In addition to understanding costs, we examined the consistency of methodological approaches applied and identified and assessed common methodological issues. Thirteen articles were identified, eight of which were from sub-Saharan Africa. Eight studies estimated costs associated with domestic or intimate partner violence, others estimated the costs of interpersonal violence, female genital cutting, and sexual assaults. Methodologies applied to estimate costs were typically based on accounting approaches. Our review found that out-of-pocket expenditures to individuals for seeking health care after an episode of violence ranged from US$29.72 (South Africa) to US$156.11 (Romania) and that lost productivity averaged from US$73.84 to US$2,151.48 (South Africa) per facility visit. Most studies that estimated provider costs of service delivery presented total programmatic costs, and there was variation in interventions, scale, and resource inputs measured which hampered comparability. Variations in methodological assumptions and data availability also made comparisons across countries and settings challenging. The limited scope of studies in measuring the multifaceted impacts of violence highlights the challenges in identifying cost metrics that extend beyond specific violence episodes. Despite the limited evidence base, our assessment leads us to conclude that the estimated costs of violence against women and girls are a fraction of its true economic burden. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
