874. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2023 to December Week 2 2023>
ID  - 2022-90096-058
T1  - Essential collaboration practices for R&D partners.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Nichols, Charles Reid
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Collaboration within the Research and Development (R&D) community brings people with specialized backgrounds together to solve complex problems. This dissertation seeks to identify innovations in management that contribute to the accomplishment of collaborative research, especially projects that include government, university, and industry partners. Compared to leadership research, investigations related to the science of collaboration are nascent in the management research community. Results from this dissertation can be applied to optimize the achievement of innovation and the implementation of solutions to complex problems. For these reasons, this study addresses the following research question: What factors influence the success of collaborative government-university-industry research and development projects? This question is answered through a systematic review that applies systems and stakeholder theories as a lens to better understand contemporary collaboration components. Thematic analysis of 42 scholarly studies identified five key success factors that affect government, university, and industry partners. Collaboration success factors included the establishment of favorable government policies and programs, expanding research networks, utilizing intermediaries, breaking down barriers through collaboration management, and ensuring co-production. These factors support team building, improve research capacity, promote trust, and engender commitment among partners. Leaders from government, academia, and industry who are working on complex problems that are beyond the scope of a single organization should consider these success factors to optimize the opportunity for R&D teams to achieve project goals and generate favorable societal impacts through collaboration. This research found a gap in information relating to viable techniques to resolve conflicts among R&D partners. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
