A1  - Timulak, Ladislav
A1  - Creaner, Mary
A3  - Timulak, Ladislav, Creaner, Mary
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - This chapter offers authors' perspectives on publishing a qualitative meta-analysis (QMA) that meta-analysts may find useful when preparing their QMA manuscript. There will obviously be some overlap with the recently published guidelines for the preparation of qualitative study manuscripts for American Psychological Association (APA) journals. These guidelines conveniently dedicate space specifically to QMAs. Given that QMA is a form of systematic review, the chapter urges readers also to consult the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines on reporting of systematic reviews. It provides some tips and suggestions for the main sections -Introduction, Method, Results, and Discussion-of a manuscript and outlines the essential information that needs to be included in each section. Research findings across samples and studies are more compelling than those from a single study, so the authors strongly encourage readers to persevere and publish their QMAs. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
