Y1  - 2022//
N2  - [Correction Notice: An Erratum for this article was reported in Vol 129(4-5) of Ethology (see record 2023-63342-003). In the originally published article, the author would like to update the Data Availability Statement. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT The data for this study are available on the Dryad repository at the following URL: https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.0vt4b8h21. The online version has been updated to reflect this change.] Temperature impacts a wide range of mating behaviors, particularly in ectothermic organisms that tend to have body temperatures similar to ambient thermal conditions. Here, we test the effects of thermal variation on precopulatory and copulatory behavior in the harvester Leiobunum politum Weed 1889, which belongs to the group commonly known as daddy longlegs. We ran single choice mating trials across temperatures commonly experienced in the field during the mating season (18-34degreeC) for 2 years. We tested how temperature affected the likelihood to move, attempt to mate, and successfully mate, as well as the duration of copulation. Mating was highest at low to intermediate temperatures, and the temperature at which peak mating rates occurred varied across years. The wide range of temperatures across which L. politum is found to mate reflects thermal variability in the field and the flexibility in mating behavior in this fascinating animal. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
