958. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2022>
ID  - 2022-45359-272
T1  - Work culture and the influence of employee on-the-job alcohol use in the restaurant industry.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Frye, Lindsay
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - The purpose of this qualitative descriptive study was to explore how restaurant employees describe the influence of work culture artifacts, espoused values, and basic assumptions on restaurant food server behavior with respect to on-the-job alcohol use in the United States. The theoretical foundation of this study was Schein's organizational work culture model, according to which artifacts, espoused values, and basic assumptions make up an organization's work culture. The study had three research questions that addressed the purpose of the study based on the three components of the model. The sample consisted of 12 participants who completed semistructured interviews and 88 participants who completed questionnaires. Data analysis consisted of thematic analysis for interview data and descriptive statistics for questionnaire data. Twelve themes emerged from the data. The results indicated that work culture artifacts, espoused values, and basic assumptions do influence food server behavior with respect to on-the-job alcohol use. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
